/* Responsibility: long rules text for the non-PHB 2014 species traits and background features. Data only. Read by 06b-data-2014-long-text.js. Own wording. Key: species name without "(2014)" then trait name; background "name|feature". */
export const MORE14_SPECIES = {
 "Astral Elf": {"Starlight Step": "Use a Bonus Action to teleport up to 30 feet to an open space you can see. You can do this a number of times equal to your Proficiency Bonus. All uses return after a Long Rest.", "Astral Trance": "You never need sleep and spells cannot put you to sleep. A Long Rest takes you only 4 hours if you spend them in a waking trance. Each time the trance ends, pick one skill and one weapon or tool from the Player's Handbook. You are proficient in both until your next Long Rest. The elves describe this as borrowing from shared elven memory and from beings on the Astral Plane.", "Astral Fire": "You know one cantrip of your choice: Dancing Lights, Light or Sacred Flame. Intelligence, Wisdom or Charisma is your spellcasting ability for it, chosen when you pick this species."},
 "Autognome": {"Armored Casing": "Your body is a shell of metal or a similar tough material. While you wear no armor, your Armor Class is 13 plus your Dexterity modifier.", "Built for Success": "After you see the d20 for one of your attack rolls, ability checks or saving throws, and before the result is applied, you may add a d4 to it. You can do this a number of times equal to your Proficiency Bonus. All uses return after a Long Rest.", "Healing Machine": "When Mending is cast on you, you may spend one Hit Die, roll it and regain Hit Points equal to the roll plus your Constitution modifier (minimum 1). Your creator also made you able to benefit from Cure Wounds, Healing Word, Mass Cure Wounds, Mass Healing Word and Spare the Dying. These normally pass over Constructs, but they work on you.", "Sentry's Rest": "You do not sleep. A Long Rest for you means at least 6 hours standing or lying inactive and still. You look lifeless in this state, yet you stay awake and aware."},
 "Giff": {"Astral Spark": "Your link to the Astral Plane lets you pass a spark of divine power through your weapons. When you hit with a simple or martial weapon, the target takes extra Force damage equal to your Proficiency Bonus. You can use this once per turn, and a number of times equal to your Proficiency Bonus. All uses return after a Long Rest.", "Firearms Mastery": "The giff's gods loved firearms, and you inherit a gift for them. You are proficient with every firearm and ignore the Loading property. Attacking at long range with a firearm does not give you Disadvantage.", "Hippo Build": "You have Advantage on Strength checks and Strength saving throws. You count as one size larger for carrying capacity and for how much you can push, drag or lift."},
 "Hadozee": {"Glide": "When you fall 10 feet or more, you can use your Reaction to spread your skin membranes. You glide horizontally up to your Speed in a direction you pick, and you take no fall damage.", "Hadozee Dodge": "When you take damage, use your Reaction, roll a d6 and add your Proficiency Bonus. Reduce the damage by that total, to a minimum of 0. You can do this a number of times equal to your Proficiency Bonus. All uses return after a Long Rest."},
 "Plasmoid": {"Shape Self": "With an action, you can form a head, one or two arms and legs, and rough hands and feet, or sink back into a limbless blob. In a humanlike shape you can wear clothing and armor made for a Humanoid of your size. With a Bonus Action you can push out a pseudopod up to 6 inches wide and 10 feet long, or pull it in. That same Bonus Action lets the pseudopod handle an object, work a door or container, or lift or set down a Tiny object. The pseudopod senses nothing, cannot attack or activate magic items, and cannot carry over 10 pounds.", "Amorphous": "You can squeeze through a space 1 inch wide, as long as you wear and carry nothing. You have Advantage on ability checks to start or escape a grapple."},
 "Thri-kreen": {"Chameleon Carapace": "Your shell gives you an Armor Class of 13 plus your Dexterity modifier while you wear no armor. As an action, you can change its color and texture to match your surroundings. You have Advantage on Dexterity (Stealth) checks to hide there.", "Secondary Arms": "A smaller second pair of arms sits below your main pair. They can handle an object, open or close a door or container, pick up or put down a Tiny object, or hold a weapon with the Light property.", "Thri-kreen Telepathy": "Without magic you cannot speak languages other than thri-kreen. You send thoughts instead. You can mentally speak to willing creatures within 120 feet. They need not share a language with you, but they must understand at least one language. The link breaks if either of you moves beyond 120 feet, either of you is Incapacitated, or either of you ends it (no action).", "Sleepless": "You do not need sleep and stay conscious during a Long Rest. You must still avoid strenuous activity to gain its benefit."},
 "Gnoll": {"Bite": "Your jaws are a natural weapon for unarmed strikes. A hit deals Piercing damage equal to 1d4 plus your Strength modifier, instead of the usual Bludgeoning.", "Darkvision": "Within 60 feet, darkness looks like Dim Light to you, and Dim Light looks like Bright Light. Colors vanish in the dark and you see only gray tones."},
 "Grimlock": {"Blindsight": "You have no eyes, so nothing can Blind you. You sense your surroundings within 30 feet, or within 10 feet while you are Deafened. Beyond that range you count as Blinded."},
 "Kuo-Toa": {"Superior Darkvision": "Your sight in poor light is exceptional. Within 120 feet, darkness looks like Dim Light to you, and Dim Light looks like Bright Light. Colors vanish in the dark and you see only gray tones.", "Otherworldly Perception": "You sense any invisible creature, or any creature on the Ethereal Plane, within 30 feet. You can pinpoint one that is moving."},
 "Skeleton": {"Darkvision": "Within 60 feet, darkness looks like Dim Light to you, and Dim Light looks like Bright Light. Colors vanish in the dark and you see only gray tones."},
 "Troglodyte": {"Darkvision": "Within 60 feet, darkness looks like Dim Light to you, and Dim Light looks like Bright Light. Colors vanish in the dark and you see only gray tones."},
 "Zombie": {"Darkvision": "Within 60 feet, darkness looks like Dim Light to you, and Dim Light looks like Bright Light. Colors vanish in the dark and you see only gray tones."},
 "Dwarf (Mark of Warding)": {"Tool Proficiency": "Pick one artisan's tool kit: smith's tools, brewer's supplies or mason's tools. You are proficient with it.", "Stonecunning": "On an Intelligence (History) check about where stonework came from, you count as proficient in History and add twice your Proficiency Bonus instead of the normal amount.", "Wards and Seals": "You can cast Alarm and Mage Armor with this trait. At level 3 you can also cast Arcane Lock. Each spell works once, then returns after a Long Rest. Intelligence is your spellcasting ability for them, and you need no Material components.", "Warder's Intuition": "Add a d4 to your Intelligence (Investigation) checks and to any check with thieves' tools. Roll the d4 and add it to the total."},
 "Elf (Mark of Shadow)": {"Trance": "You do not sleep. You meditate semiconscious for 4 hours a day instead. The Common word for it is trance. While meditating you may dream after a fashion, which is really practiced mental exercise. You get the same benefit a human gets from 8 hours of sleep. A Long Rest taken this way ends after 4 hours. All other Long Rest rules still apply.", "Cunning Intuition": "Add a d4 to your Charisma (Performance) and Dexterity (Stealth) checks. Roll the d4 and add it to the total.", "Shape Shadows": "You know the Minor Illusion cantrip. At level 3 you can cast Invisibility once with this trait and regain it after a Long Rest. Charisma is your spellcasting ability for these spells."},
 "Gnome (Mark of Scribing)": {"Gifted Scribe": "When you make an Intelligence (History) check, or any ability check with calligrapher's supplies, you can roll a d4 and add it to the result.", "Scribe's Insight": "You know the Message cantrip. You can cast Comprehend Languages once and regain it after a Short or Long Rest. At level 3 you can cast Magic Mouth once and regain it after a Long Rest. Intelligence is your spellcasting ability for these spells."},
 "Halfling (Mark of Healing)": {"Medical Intuition": "When you make a Wisdom (Medicine) check, or any ability check with an herbalism kit, you can roll a d4 and add it to the result.", "Healing Touch": "You can cast Cure Wounds with this trait. At level 3 you can also cast Lesser Restoration. Each spell works once, then returns after a Long Rest. Wisdom is your spellcasting ability for them."},
 "Halfling (Mark of Hospitality)": {"Ever Hospitable": "When you make a Charisma (Persuasion) check, or any ability check with brewer's supplies or cook's utensils, you can roll a d4 and add it to the result.", "Innkeeper's Magic": "You know the Prestidigitation cantrip. You can also cast Purify Food and Drink and Unseen Servant with this trait. Each spell works once, then returns after a Long Rest. Charisma is your spellcasting ability for them."},
 "Human (Mark of Handling)": {"Languages": "You speak, read and write Common plus one extra language you choose. Humans pick up the tongues of the peoples they meet, and they mix borrowed words into their own speech.", "Wild Intuition": "Add a d4 to your Wisdom (Animal Handling) and Intelligence (Nature) checks. Roll the d4 and add it to the total.", "Primal Connection": "You can cast Animal Friendship and Speak with Animals with this trait, with no Material component. Each spell works once, then returns after a Short or Long Rest. Wisdom is your spellcasting ability for them.", "The Bigger They Are": "From level 3, Animal Friendship and Speak with Animals can target a Beast or Monstrosity whose Intelligence score is 3 or lower."},
 "Human (Mark of Making)": {"Languages": "You speak, read and write Common plus one extra language you choose. Humans pick up the tongues of the peoples they meet, and they mix borrowed words into their own speech.", "Artisan's Intuition": "When you make an Arcana check, or any ability check with artisan's tools, you can roll a d4 and add it to the result.", "Spellsmith": "You know the Mending cantrip. You can cast Magic Weapon with this trait. It lasts 1 hour and needs no Concentration. You can do this once, then again after a Long Rest. Intelligence is your spellcasting ability for these spells."},
 "Human (Mark of Passage)": {"Languages": "You speak, read and write Common plus one extra language you choose. Humans pick up the tongues of the peoples they meet, and they mix borrowed words into their own speech.", "Intuitive Motion": "Add a d4 to your Dexterity (Acrobatics) checks and to any check you make to drive or maintain a land vehicle. Roll the d4 and add it to the total.", "Magical Passage": "You can cast Misty Step once with this trait and regain it after a Long Rest. Dexterity is your spellcasting ability for it."},
 "Human (Mark of Sentinel)": {"Languages": "You speak, read and write Common plus one extra language you choose. Humans pick up the tongues of the peoples they meet, and they mix borrowed words into their own speech.", "Sentinel's Intuition": "Add a d4 to your Wisdom (Insight) and Wisdom (Perception) checks. Roll the d4 and add it to the total.", "Guardian's Shield": "You can cast Shield once with this trait and regain it after a Long Rest. Wisdom is your spellcasting ability for it.", "Vigilant Guardian": "If you see a creature within 5 feet of you struck by an attack roll, you can spend your Reaction to trade places with it, so the attack hits you. This works once, then recharges on a Long Rest."},
 "Changeling": {"Shapechanger": "As an action, you change your looks and voice. You decide the details, such as coloring, hair length and sex. You can adjust height and weight, but not enough to change your size. You can appear as a member of another race, but your game statistics stay the same. You cannot copy a creature you have never seen, and the new form must have the same basic arrangement of limbs as yours. Your clothing and gear do not change. The form lasts until you use an action to return to your true form, or until you die.", "Changeling Instincts": "Pick two of Deception, Insight, Intimidation and Persuasion. You are proficient in both."},
 "Kalashtar": {"Mind Link": "You can speak telepathically to any creature you can see within 10 feet times your level. It need not share a language with you, but it must understand at least one. While you do this, you can use your action to let that creature answer you telepathically for 1 hour, or until you end it with an action. It must see you and stay within range. Only one creature at a time can have this ability. Giving it to a new one removes it from the old one.", "Severed from Dreams": "Kalashtar do sleep, but they do not link to the plane of dreams. Their minds draw on the memories of their spirit instead. You are immune to spells and effects that need you to dream, such as Dream. You are not immune to those that put you to sleep, such as Sleep."},
 "Warforged": {"Constructed Resilience": "You have Advantage on saves against the Poisoned condition and Resistance to Poison damage. You do not eat, drink or breathe. You are immune to disease. You do not sleep, and magic cannot put you to sleep.", "Sentry's Rest": "A Long Rest for you means at least 6 hours inactive and motionless, not asleep. You look inert, but you are not Unconscious. You see and hear normally.", "Integrated Protection": "Your body has built-in armor layers and gives you a +1 bonus to Armor Class. You can only don armor you are proficient with. To don armor other than a shield, you fuse it to your body over 1 hour, staying in contact with it. Taking it off also takes 1 hour. You can rest during either process. While you live, nobody can remove fused armor against your will."},
 "Shifter (Beasthide)": {"Shifting": "As a Bonus Action, you take on a beastlike look for 1 minute. It ends early if you die or choose to end it with a Bonus Action. Shifting gives you Temporary Hit Points equal to your level plus your Constitution modifier (minimum 1). Your shifter subrace adds more benefits while shifted. After one use you need a Short or Long Rest to shift again.", "Shifting Feature": "Each time you shift, you gain an extra 1d6 Temporary Hit Points. While shifted, you have a +1 bonus to Armor Class."},
 "Shifter (Longtooth)": {"Shifting": "As a Bonus Action, you take on a beastlike look for 1 minute. It ends early if you die or choose to end it with a Bonus Action. Shifting gives you Temporary Hit Points equal to your level plus your Constitution modifier (minimum 1). Your shifter subrace adds more benefits while shifted. After one use you need a Short or Long Rest to shift again.", "Shifting Feature": "While shifted, you can make an unarmed strike with your long fangs as a Bonus Action. A hit deals Piercing damage equal to 1d6 plus your Strength modifier, instead of the usual Bludgeoning."},
 "Shifter (Swiftstride)": {"Shifting": "As a Bonus Action, you take on a beastlike look for 1 minute. It ends early if you die or choose to end it with a Bonus Action. Shifting gives you Temporary Hit Points equal to your level plus your Constitution modifier (minimum 1). Your shifter subrace adds more benefits while shifted. After one use you need a Short or Long Rest to shift again.", "Shifting Feature": "While shifted, your Speed rises by 10 feet. You can also use your Reaction to move up to 10 feet when a creature ends its turn within 5 feet of you. This movement does not provoke Opportunity Attacks."},
 "Shifter (Wildhunt)": {"Shifting": "As a Bonus Action, you take on a beastlike look for 1 minute. It ends early if you die or choose to end it with a Bonus Action. Shifting gives you Temporary Hit Points equal to your level plus your Constitution modifier (minimum 1). Your shifter subrace adds more benefits while shifted. After one use you need a Short or Long Rest to shift again."},
 "Aarakocra": {"Talons": "Your talons are natural weapons for unarmed strikes. A hit deals Slashing damage equal to 1d4 plus your Strength modifier, instead of the usual Bludgeoning.", "Flight": "Your wings give you a Fly Speed equal to your Speed. You cannot fly while you wear Medium or Heavy armor."},
 "Centaur": {"Charge": "After you move 30 feet or more in a straight line at a target and hit it with a melee weapon attack in the same turn, you can spend a Bonus Action to strike it once with your hooves.", "Hooves": "Your hooves are natural weapons for unarmed strikes. A hit deals Bludgeoning damage equal to 1d4 plus your Strength modifier.", "Equine Build": "You count as one size larger for carrying capacity and for how much you can push or drag. Climbing anything that needs hands and feet is hard on your horse legs. Each foot of such a climb costs 4 extra feet of movement, not 1.", "Survivor": "Pick one of Animal Handling, Medicine, Nature and Survival. You are proficient in it."},
 "Loxodon": {"Natural Armor": "Your thick hide gives you AC 12 plus your Constitution modifier while you wear no armor. You may use it whenever the armor you wear would give a lower AC. A shield still adds its bonus. When two features each give a way to work out AC, you pick one and do not combine them. For example, choose either this or a Monk's Unarmored Defense.", "Trunk": "Your trunk can grasp objects and works as a snorkel. Its reach is 5 feet and it lifts a number of pounds equal to five times your Strength score. It can lift, drop, hold, push or pull an object or creature, open or close a door or container, grapple, or make an unarmed strike. Your DM may allow other simple tasks. It cannot wield weapons or shields, or do anything needing fine control, such as using tools or magic items or making a spell's somatic gestures.", "Keen Smell": "Your sensitive trunk gives you Advantage on Wisdom (Perception), Wisdom (Survival) and Intelligence (Investigation) checks that involve smell."},
 "Minotaur": {"Horns": "Your horns are natural weapons for unarmed strikes. A hit deals Piercing damage equal to 1d6 plus your Strength modifier, instead of the usual Bludgeoning.", "Hammering Horns": "After you hit with a melee attack during the Attack action on your turn, you can spend a Bonus Action to butt the target with your horns. The target must be within 5 feet and at most one size larger than you. It makes a Strength save against DC 8 plus your Proficiency Bonus plus your Strength modifier. If it fails, you push it up to 10 feet away from you."},
 "Simic Hybrid": {"Animal Enhancement": "Your body carries animal traits. Choose one enhancement at level 1 and a second at level 5. Level 1 choices. Manta Glide: ray-like fins slow a fall. While not Incapacitated you subtract up to 100 feet from the fall when working out damage, and you move 2 feet sideways for each foot you descend. Nimble Climber: you get a Climb Speed equal to your Speed. Underwater Adaptation: you breathe air and water and get a Swim Speed equal to your Speed. At level 5 pick one you did not take, or one of these. Grappling Appendage: two limbs grow beside your arms, each a claw or tentacle (your choice). As an action you try to grapple with one. Each is a natural weapon for unarmed strikes dealing Bludgeoning damage equal to 1d6 plus your Strength modifier. After a hit you can try to grapple as a Bonus Action. They cannot do fine work or wield weapons, magic items or other gear. Carapace: patches of thick shell give you +1 AC while you do not wear Heavy armor. Acid Spit: as an action, spray acid at a creature or object you see within 30 feet. The target makes a Dexterity save against DC 8 plus your Constitution modifier plus your Proficiency Bonus. On a failure it takes 2d10 Acid damage, 3d10 at level 11, 4d10 at level 17. You can use it a number of times equal to your Constitution modifier (minimum 1). All uses return after a Long Rest."},
 "Vedalken": {"Tireless Precision": "Pick one of Arcana, History, Investigation, Medicine, Performance and Sleight of Hand, and also one tool. You are proficient in both. Whenever you make an ability check with that skill or tool, roll a d4 and add it to the total.", "Partially Amphibious": "You absorb oxygen through your skin and can breathe underwater for up to 1 hour. After that you must finish a Long Rest before using this trait again."},
 "Leonin": {"Claws": "Your claws are natural weapons for unarmed strikes. A hit deals Slashing damage equal to 1d4 plus your Strength modifier, instead of the usual Bludgeoning.", "Hunter's Instincts": "Pick one of Athletics, Intimidation, Perception and Survival. You are proficient in it.", "Daunting Roar": "With a Bonus Action you let out a terrifying roar. Each creature you choose within 10 feet that hears it makes a Wisdom save or is Frightened of you until the end of your next turn. The DC is 8 plus your Proficiency Bonus plus your Constitution modifier. This works once, then recharges on a Short or Long Rest."},
 "Satyr": {"Ram": "You can make unarmed strikes with your head and horns. A hit deals Bludgeoning damage equal to 1d4 plus your Strength modifier.", "Reveler": "You are proficient in Performance and Persuasion, and with one musical instrument you choose.", "Mirthful Leaps": "When you make a long jump or high jump, roll a d8 and add it to the distance in feet, even from a standing start. The extra distance costs movement as normal."},
 "Eladrin": {"Trance": "You do not sleep. You meditate semiconscious for 4 hours a day instead. The Common word for it is trance. While meditating you may dream after a fashion, which is really practiced mental exercise. You get the same benefit a human gets from 8 hours of sleep. A Long Rest taken this way ends after 4 hours. All other Long Rest rules still apply.", "Fey Step": "As a Bonus Action, teleport up to 30 feet to an open space you can see. You can do this once, then again after a Short or Long Rest. At level 3 the step gains an extra effect set by your season. Any save uses DC 8 plus your Proficiency Bonus plus your Charisma modifier. Autumn: right after the step, up to two creatures you choose within 10 feet make a Wisdom save or are Charmed by you for 1 minute, ending early if you or your allies damage them. Winter: when you step, one creature you can see within 5 feet before you leave makes a Wisdom save or is Frightened of you until the end of your next turn. Spring: when you step, you can touch one willing creature within 5 feet. It teleports instead of you, to an open space of its choice within 30 feet of you that you can see. Summer: right after the step, each creature you choose within 5 feet takes Fire damage equal to your Charisma modifier, minimum 1."},
 "Sea Elf": {"Trance": "You do not sleep and magic cannot put you to sleep. A Long Rest takes you 4 hours if you spend them in a waking trance. Each time the trance ends, you gain two proficiencies you lack, each with a weapon or tool from the Player's Handbook. They come from shared elven memory and last until your next Long Rest.", "Friend of the Sea": "Sea creatures feel a strong bond with your people. You can pass simple ideas to any Beast with a Swim Speed. It understands your words. You get no special ability to understand it back."},
 "Shadar-kai": {"Trance": "You do not sleep. You meditate semiconscious for 4 hours a day instead. The Common word for it is trance. While meditating you may dream after a fashion, which is really practiced mental exercise. You get the same benefit a human gets from 8 hours of sleep. A Long Rest taken this way ends after 4 hours. All other Long Rest rules still apply.", "Blessing of the Raven Queen": "As a Bonus Action, teleport up to 30 feet to an open space you can see. You can do this once, then again after a Long Rest. From level 3 you also have Resistance to all damage until the start of your next turn after you teleport this way. You look ghostly and translucent during that time."},
 "Duergar": {"Superior Darkvision": "Life underground gave you exceptional sight in poor light. Within 120 feet, darkness looks like Dim Light to you, and Dim Light looks like Bright Light. Colors vanish in the dark and you see only gray tones.", "Duergar Resilience": "You have Advantage on saving throws against poison, illusions, being Charmed and being Paralyzed. You also have Resistance to Poison damage.", "Duergar Magic": "At level 3 you can cast Enlarge/Reduce on yourself once, using only the enlarge option. At level 5 you can cast Invisibility on yourself once. Neither needs Material components. You cannot cast them in direct sunlight, but sunlight does not affect them once cast. Both return after a Long Rest. Intelligence is your spellcasting ability for them.", "Tool Proficiency": "Pick one artisan's tool kit: smith's tools, brewer's supplies or mason's tools. You are proficient with it.", "Stonecunning": "On an Intelligence (History) check about where stonework came from, you count as proficient in History and add twice your Proficiency Bonus instead of the normal amount.", "Sunlight Sensitivity": "In direct sunlight, you have Disadvantage on attack rolls and on sight-based Wisdom (Perception) checks. This applies when you, your target or the thing you try to notice stands in the sun."},
 "Githyanki": {"Decadent Mastery": "You learn one language and gain proficiency in one skill or tool, all of your choice. In Tu'narath, the timeless city, githyanki have endless time to master odd pieces of knowledge.", "Githyanki Psionics": "You know Mage Hand, and the hand you conjure with this trait is unseen. At level 3 you can cast Jump once, and at level 5 Misty Step once. Each recharges on a Long Rest. Intelligence is the spellcasting ability, and you need no components."},
 "Githzerai": {"Mental Discipline": "You have Advantage on saves against being Charmed or Frightened. Monastic teachers train githzerai to rule their own minds.", "Githzerai Psionics": "You know Mage Hand, and the hand you conjure with this trait is unseen. At level 3 you can cast Shield once, and at level 5 Detect Thoughts once. Each recharges on a Long Rest. Wisdom is the spellcasting ability, and you need no components."},
 "Tiefling (Baalzebul)": {"Legacy of Maladomini": "You know the Thaumaturgy cantrip. At level 3 you can cast Ray of Sickness once as a level 2 spell. At level 5 you can cast Crown of Madness once. Each returns after a Long Rest. Charisma is your spellcasting ability for them."},
 "Tiefling (Dispater)": {"Legacy of Dis": "You know the Thaumaturgy cantrip. At level 3 you can cast Disguise Self once. At level 5 you can cast Detect Thoughts once. Each returns after a Long Rest. Charisma is your spellcasting ability for them."},
 "Tiefling (Fierna)": {"Legacy of Phlegethos": "You know the Friends cantrip. At level 3 you can cast Charm Person once as a level 2 spell. At level 5 you can cast Suggestion once. Each returns after a Long Rest. Charisma is your spellcasting ability for them."},
 "Tiefling (Glasya)": {"Legacy of Malbolge": "You know the Minor Illusion cantrip. At level 3 you can cast Disguise Self once. At level 5 you can cast Invisibility once. Each returns after a Long Rest. Charisma is your spellcasting ability for them."},
 "Tiefling (Levistus)": {"Legacy of Stygia": "You know the Ray of Frost cantrip. At level 3 you can cast Armor of Agathys once as a level 2 spell. At level 5 you can cast Darkness once. Each returns after a Long Rest. Charisma is your spellcasting ability for them."},
 "Tiefling (Mammon)": {"Legacy of Minauros": "You know the Mage Hand cantrip. At level 3 you can cast Tenser's Floating Disk once and regain it after a Short or Long Rest. At level 5 you can cast Arcane Lock once, with no Material component, and regain it after a Long Rest. Charisma is your spellcasting ability for them."},
 "Tiefling (Mephistopheles)": {"Legacy of Cania": "You know the Mage Hand cantrip. At level 3 you can cast Burning Hands once as a level 2 spell. At level 5 you can cast Flame Blade once. Each returns after a Long Rest. Charisma is your spellcasting ability for them."},
 "Tiefling (Zariel)": {"Legacy of Avernus": "You know the Thaumaturgy cantrip. At level 3 you can cast Searing Smite once as a level 2 spell. At level 5 you can cast Branding Smite once. Each returns after a Long Rest. Charisma is your spellcasting ability for them."},
 "Dragonborn (Chromatic)": {"Breath Weapon": "When you take the Attack action, you can swap one attack for a breath of magical energy in a 30-foot line, 5 feet wide. Each creature in it makes a Dexterity save against DC 8 plus your Constitution modifier plus your Proficiency Bonus. On a failure it takes 1d10 damage of your Chromatic Ancestry type, and half on a success. The damage becomes 2d10 at level 5, 3d10 at level 11 and 4d10 at level 17. You can use it a number of times equal to your Proficiency Bonus. All uses return after a Long Rest.", "Chromatic Warding": "From level 5, use an action to channel draconic energy. For 1 minute you are immune to the damage type of your Chromatic Ancestry. You can do this once, then again after a Long Rest.", "Chromatic Ancestry": "A chromatic dragon is among your ancestors, which gives you a magical affinity. Choose one kind of dragon from the Chromatic Ancestry table. It sets the damage type for your other traits."},
 "Dragonborn (Gem)": {"Breath Weapon": "When you take the Attack action, you can swap one attack for a breath of magical energy in a 15-foot cone. Each creature in it makes a Dexterity save against DC 8 plus your Constitution modifier plus your Proficiency Bonus. On a failure it takes 1d10 damage of your Gem Ancestry type, and half on a success. The damage becomes 2d10 at level 5, 3d10 at level 11 and 4d10 at level 17. You can use it a number of times equal to your Proficiency Bonus. All uses return after a Long Rest.", "Psionic Mind": "You can send telepathic messages to any creature you see within 30 feet. It need not share a language with you, but it must understand at least one language.", "Gem Flight": "From level 5, use a Bonus Action to grow spectral wings for 1 minute. You gain a Fly Speed equal to your Speed and can hover. You can do this once, then again after a Long Rest."},
 "Dragonborn (Metallic)": {"Breath Weapon": "When you take the Attack action, you can swap one attack for a breath of magical energy in a 15-foot cone. Each creature in it makes a Dexterity save against DC 8 plus your Constitution modifier plus your Proficiency Bonus. On a failure it takes 1d10 damage of your Metallic Ancestry type, and half on a success. The damage becomes 2d10 at level 5, 3d10 at level 11 and 4d10 at level 17. You can use it a number of times equal to your Proficiency Bonus. All uses return after a Long Rest.", "Metallic Breath Weapon": "At level 5 you gain a second breath. When you take the Attack action, you can swap one attack for a 15-foot cone. The save DC is 8 plus your Constitution modifier plus your Proficiency Bonus. Each time, pick one effect. Enervating Breath: creatures in the cone make a Constitution save or are Incapacitated until the start of your next turn. Repulsion Breath: creatures in the cone make a Strength save or are pushed 20 feet away from you and knocked Prone. You can use this breath once, then again after a Long Rest.", "Metallic Ancestry": "A metallic dragon is among your ancestors, which gives you a magical affinity. Choose one kind of dragon from the Metallic Ancestry table. It sets the damage type for your other traits."},
 "Custom Lineage": {"Variable Trait": "Choose one: Darkvision out to 60 feet, or proficiency in one skill of your choice.", "Languages": "You speak, read and write Common and one more language that you and your DM agree fits your character."},
 "Kender": {"Fearless": "You have Advantage on saves to avoid or end the Frightened condition on yourself. When you fail such a save, you may choose to succeed instead. You can do this once, then again after a Long Rest.", "Kender Curiosity": "Your people's magical origin gives you proficiency in one of Insight, Investigation, Sleight of Hand, Stealth and Survival, your choice.", "Taunt": "As a Bonus Action, you fire off provoking words at a creature within 60 feet that can hear and understand you. It makes a Wisdom save. On a failure it has Disadvantage on attack rolls against anyone but you until the start of your next turn. The DC is 8 plus your Proficiency Bonus plus your Intelligence, Wisdom or Charisma modifier, picked when you choose this species. You can use it a number of times equal to your Proficiency Bonus. All uses return after a Long Rest."},
 "Owlin": {"Flight": "Your wings give you a Fly Speed equal to your Speed. You cannot fly while you wear Medium or Heavy armor."},
 "Verdan": {"Black Blood Healing": "Your dark blood, a mark of your people's tie to That-Which-Endures, aids recovery. When a Hit Die you spend at the end of a Short Rest shows a 1 or 2, you may reroll it, and you keep the new result.", "Limited Telepathy": "You can speak telepathically to any creature you see within 30 feet. It need not share a language with you, but it must understand at least one. The exchange is slow and carries only simple ideas.", "Telepathic Insight": "You have Advantage on all Wisdom and Charisma saving throws."},
 "Grung": {"Poisonous Skin": "A creature that grapples you or touches your skin directly makes a DC 12 Constitution save or becomes Poisoned for 1 minute. If it stops touching you, it repeats the save at the end of each of its turns and ends the effect on a success. You can also coat a piercing weapon with this poison as part of an attack with it. On a hit, the target makes a DC 12 Constitution save or takes 2d4 Poison damage.", "Water Dependency": "If you do not soak in water for at least 1 hour during a day, you gain one level of Exhaustion at the end of that day. Only magic, or soaking in water for at least 1 hour, removes that Exhaustion."},
 "Locathah": {"Natural Armor": "Your scaly hide gives you AC 12 plus your Dexterity modifier while you wear no armor. You may use it whenever the armor you wear would give a lower AC. A shield still adds its bonus."},
 "Tortle": {"Claws": "Your claws are natural weapons for unarmed strikes. A hit deals Slashing damage equal to 1d4 plus your Strength modifier, instead of the usual Bludgeoning.", "Hold Breath": "You can hold your breath for up to 1 hour. Tortles do not swim well, but they can stay under water a long while.", "Natural Armor": "Your shell and body shape make armor a poor fit, but the shell already protects you well. Your AC is 17 and your Dexterity modifier does not change it. Worn armor gives you no benefit. A shield still adds its bonus.", "Shell Defense": "As an action, you pull into your shell. Until you come out, you gain +4 AC and Advantage on Strength and Constitution saves. While in the shell, you are Prone, your Speed is 0 and cannot rise, you have Disadvantage on Dexterity saves, you cannot take Reactions, and your only action is a Bonus Action to come out."},
 "Dwarf (Kaladesh)": {"Artisan's Expertise": "You are proficient with two kinds of artisan's tools you choose, and your Proficiency Bonus is doubled on checks that use either. On an Intelligence (History) check about the origin of any architectural construction, you count as proficient in History and add twice your Proficiency Bonus. This covers buildings, public works such as canals and aqueducts, and the cogwork under much of Ghirapur."},
 "Aven (Hawk-Headed)": {"Flight": "You have a Fly Speed of 30 feet. You cannot fly while you wear Medium or Heavy armor. If your campaign uses the encumbrance variant, you cannot fly while Encumbered.", "Hawkeyed": "You are proficient in Perception. Attacking at long range does not give you Disadvantage on ranged weapon attack rolls."},
 "Aven (Ibis-Headed)": {"Flight": "You have a Fly Speed of 30 feet. You cannot fly while you wear Medium or Heavy armor. If your campaign uses the encumbrance variant, you cannot fly while Encumbered."},
 "Minotaur (Amonkhet)": {"Natural Weapon": "Your horns are a natural weapon for unarmed strikes. A hit deals Bludgeoning damage equal to 1d6 plus your Strength modifier.", "Relentless Endurance": "When damage reduces you to 0 Hit Points without killing you outright, you drop to 1 Hit Point instead. You can do this once, then again after a Long Rest.", "Savage Attacks": "When you score a critical hit with a melee weapon attack, you roll one of the weapon's damage dice one extra time and add it to the critical hit's extra damage."},
 "Naga": {"Speed Burst": "Drop low and push with your arms to move faster. As a Bonus Action on your turn, with both hands free, you add 5 feet to your Speed until the end of that turn.", "Natural Weapons": "Your fanged mouth and coiling body are natural weapons for unarmed strikes. Bite: a hit deals Piercing damage equal to 1d4 plus your Strength modifier, and the target makes a Constitution save (DC 8 plus your Proficiency Bonus plus your Constitution modifier) or takes 1d4 Poison damage. Constrict: a hit deals Bludgeoning damage equal to 1d6 plus your Strength modifier and Grapples the target (escape DC 8 plus your Proficiency Bonus plus your Strength modifier). While the grapple lasts, the target is Restrained and you cannot constrict another creature."},
 "Goblin (Ixalan)": {"Grit": "You have Resistance to Fire and Psychic damage. While you wear no armor, your AC is 11 plus your Dexterity modifier.", "Agile Climber": "You have a Climb Speed of 25 feet. You cannot use it while you wear Medium or Heavy armor. If your campaign uses the encumbrance variant, you cannot use it while Encumbered."},
 "Siren": {"Flight": "You have a Fly Speed of 30 feet. You cannot fly while you wear Medium or Heavy armor. If your campaign uses the encumbrance variant, you cannot fly while Encumbered."},
 "Vampire (Ixalan)": {"Feast of Blood": "After you drain blood with your Bloodthirst ability, a surge of vigor raises your Speed by 10 feet and gives you Advantage on Strength and Dexterity checks and saving throws for 1 minute."},
 "Aasimar (Protector)": {"Healing Hands": "As an action, touch a creature. It regains Hit Points equal to your level. You can do this once, then again after a Long Rest.", "Radiant Soul": "From level 3, use an action to release your inner divine energy. Your eyes glow and two luminous, intangible wings grow. It lasts 1 minute or until you end it as a Bonus Action. You gain a Fly Speed of 30 feet. Once on each of your turns, when you damage a target with an attack or spell, you add Radiant damage equal to your level. You can do this once, then again after a Long Rest."},
 "Aasimar (Scourge)": {"Healing Hands": "As an action, touch a creature. It regains Hit Points equal to your level. You can do this once, then again after a Long Rest.", "Radiant Consumption": "From level 3, use an action to release your inner divine energy as searing light that pours from your eyes and mouth and threatens to burn you. It lasts 1 minute or until you end it as a Bonus Action. You shed Bright Light in 10 feet and Dim Light 10 feet beyond. At the end of each of your turns, you and every creature within 10 feet take Radiant damage equal to half your level, rounded up. Once on each of your turns, when you damage a target with an attack or spell, you add Radiant damage equal to your level. You can do this once, then again after a Long Rest."},
 "Aasimar (Fallen)": {"Healing Hands": "As an action, touch a creature. It regains Hit Points equal to your level. You can do this once, then again after a Long Rest.", "Necrotic Shroud": "From level 3, use an action to release your inner divine energy. Your eyes become pools of darkness and two skeletal, ghostly wings that cannot fly grow. When you transform, other creatures within 10 feet that can see you make a Charisma save (DC 8 plus your Proficiency Bonus plus your Charisma modifier) or are Frightened of you until the end of your next turn. It lasts 1 minute or until you end it as a Bonus Action. Once on each of your turns, when you damage a target with an attack or spell, you add Necrotic damage equal to your level. You can do this once, then again after a Long Rest."},
 "Bugbear": {"Surprise Attack": "If you surprise a creature and hit it on your first turn of combat, the attack deals an extra 2d6 damage. You can use this only once per combat."},
 "Firbolg": {"Firbolg Magic": "Detect Magic and Disguise Self are yours to cast, with Wisdom as the spellcasting ability. Each can be cast once, then recharges on a Short or Long Rest. Your Disguise Self can shave up to 3 feet off your apparent height, so you can pass among humans and elves.", "Hidden Step": "With a Bonus Action you turn invisible. It lasts until the start of your next turn, or until you attack, roll damage or make a creature save. This works once, then recharges on a Short or Long Rest.", "Speech of Beast and Leaf": "You can communicate in a limited way with Beasts and Plants. They understand your meaning, but you gain no special ability to understand them. You have Advantage on all Charisma checks to influence them."},
 "Goblin": {"Fury of the Small": "When you damage a creature larger than you with an attack or spell, you can add extra damage equal to your level. You can do this once, then again after a Short or Long Rest."},
 "Goliath": {"Stone's Endurance": "When you take damage, use your Reaction, roll a d12 and add your Constitution modifier. Reduce the damage by that total. You can do this once, then again after a Short or Long Rest.", "Powerful Build": "You have Advantage on ability checks to end the Grappled condition. You count as one size larger for carrying capacity.", "Mountain Born": "You have Resistance to Cold damage. You are also used to high altitude, including heights above 20,000 feet."},
 "Hobgoblin": {"Saving Face": "Hobgoblins avoid showing weakness in front of allies, because it costs them status. When you miss with an attack roll or fail an ability check or saving throw, you can add a bonus to that roll equal to the number of allies you see within 30 feet, up to +5. You can do this once, then again after a Short or Long Rest."},
 "Kenku": {"Expert Forgery": "You can copy another creature's handwriting and craftwork. You have Advantage on all checks to make a forgery or duplicate of an existing object.", "Kenku Training": "Pick two of Acrobatics, Deception, Stealth and Sleight of Hand. You are proficient in both."},
 "Kobold": {"Grovel, Cower, and Beg": "Use an action on your turn to cringe and plead, pulling foes' attention to you. Until the end of your next turn, allies have Advantage on attack rolls against enemies you see within 10 feet of you. This works once, then recharges on a Short or Long Rest.", "Pack Tactics": "You have Advantage on an attack roll against a creature when at least one of your allies is within 5 feet of it and that ally is not Incapacitated.", "Sunlight Sensitivity": "In direct sunlight, you have Disadvantage on attack rolls and on sight-based Wisdom (Perception) checks. This applies when you, your target or the thing you try to notice stands in the sun."},
 "Lizardfolk": {"Bite": "Your jaws are a natural weapon for unarmed strikes. A hit deals Piercing damage equal to 1d6 plus your Strength modifier, instead of the usual Bludgeoning.", "Cunning Artisan": "During a Short Rest, you can harvest bone and hide from a dead Beast, Construct, Dragon, Monstrosity or Plant of Small size or larger. From it you make one shield, club, javelin, or 1d4 darts or blowgun needles. You need a blade such as a dagger, or suitable artisan's tools such as leatherworker's tools.", "Hunter's Lore": "Pick two of Animal Handling, Nature, Perception, Stealth and Survival. You are proficient in both.", "Natural Armor": "Your scaly hide gives you AC 13 plus your Dexterity modifier while you wear no armor. You may use it whenever the armor you wear would give a lower AC. A shield still adds its bonus.", "Hungry Jaws": "In a feeding frenzy, you can use a Bonus Action to bite as a special attack. On a hit it deals its usual damage, and you gain Temporary Hit Points equal to your Constitution modifier, minimum 1. After one use you need a Short or Long Rest to use it again."},
 "Tabaxi": {"Feline Agility": "When you move during your turn in combat, you can double your Speed until that turn ends. To use it again, you must first take a turn in which you move 0 feet.", "Cat's Claws": "Your claws give you a Climb Speed of 20 feet. They are also natural weapons for unarmed strikes. A hit deals Slashing damage equal to 1d4 plus your Strength modifier, instead of the usual Bludgeoning."},
 "Triton": {"Control Air and Water": "You can call on elemental air and water. You can cast Fog Cloud with this trait. At level 3 you can cast Gust of Wind, and at level 5 Wall of Water. Each spell works once, then returns after a Long Rest. Charisma is your spellcasting ability for them.", "Emissary of the Sea": "Water-breathing Beasts feel a strong bond with your people. You can pass simple ideas to them. They understand your meaning, but you gain no special ability to understand them."},
 "Yuan-ti Pureblood": {"Innate Spellcasting": "You know the Poison Spray cantrip. You can cast Animal Friendship as often as you like, but only on snakes. At level 3 you can also cast Suggestion once and regain it after a Long Rest. Charisma is your spellcasting ability for them."},
 "Aasimar (Monster)": {"Darkvision": "Your celestial blood gives you sharp sight in poor light. Within 60 feet, darkness looks like Dim Light to you, and Dim Light looks like Bright Light. Colors vanish in the dark and you see only gray tones.", "Celestial Legacy": "You know the Light cantrip. At level 3 you can cast Lesser Restoration once, and at level 5 Daylight once. Each returns after a Long Rest. Charisma is your spellcasting ability for them."},
 "Deep Gnome (Monster)": {"Superior Darkvision": "Life underground gave you exceptional sight in poor light. Within 120 feet, darkness looks like Dim Light to you, and Dim Light looks like Bright Light. Colors vanish in the dark and you see only gray tones."},
 "Goblin (Monster)": {"Darkvision": "Life underground gave you sharp sight in poor light. Within 60 feet, darkness looks like Dim Light to you, and Dim Light looks like Bright Light. Colors vanish in the dark and you see only gray tones."},
 "Hobgoblin (Monster)": {"Darkvision": "Within 60 feet, darkness looks like Dim Light to you, and Dim Light looks like Bright Light. Colors vanish in the dark and you see only gray tones."},
 "Kobold (Monster)": {"Sunlight Sensitivity": "In direct sunlight, you have Disadvantage on attack rolls and on sight-based Wisdom (Perception) checks. This applies when you, your target or the thing you try to notice stands in the sun.", "Darkvision": "Life underground gave you sharp sight in poor light. Within 60 feet, darkness looks like Dim Light to you, and Dim Light looks like Bright Light. Colors vanish in the dark and you see only gray tones."},
 "Orc (Monster)": {"Darkvision": "Life underground gave you sharp sight in poor light. Within 60 feet, darkness looks like Dim Light to you, and Dim Light looks like Bright Light. Colors vanish in the dark and you see only gray tones."},
 "Eladrin Elf": {"Trance": "You do not sleep. You meditate semiconscious for 4 hours a day instead. The Common word for it is trance. While meditating you may dream after a fashion, which is really practiced mental exercise. You get the same benefit a human gets from 8 hours of sleep. A Long Rest taken this way ends after 4 hours. All other Long Rest rules still apply."},
 "Half-Elf (Mark of Detection)": {"Deductive Intuition": "When you make an Intelligence (Investigation) or Wisdom (Insight) check, you can roll a d4 and add it to the result.", "Magical Detection": "You can cast Detect Magic and Detect Poison and Disease with this trait. At level 3 you can also cast See Invisibility. Each spell works once, then returns after a Long Rest. Wisdom is your spellcasting ability for them, and you need no Material components."},
 "Half-Elf (Mark of Storm)": {"Windwright's Intuition": "When you make a Dexterity (Acrobatics) check, or any ability check with navigator's tools, you can roll a d4 and add it to the result.", "Headwinds": "You know the Gust cantrip. At level 3 you can cast Gust of Wind once and regain it after a Long Rest. Charisma is your spellcasting ability for these spells."},
 "Half-Orc (Mark of Finding)": {"Hunter's Intuition": "Add a d4 to your Wisdom (Perception) and Wisdom (Survival) checks. Roll the d4 and add it to the total.", "Finder's Magic": "You can cast Hunter's Mark with this trait. At level 3 you can also cast Locate Object. Each spell works once, then returns after a Long Rest. Wisdom is your spellcasting ability for them."},
 "Human (Mark of Finding)": {"Hunter's Intuition": "Add a d4 to your Wisdom (Perception) and Wisdom (Survival) checks. Roll the d4 and add it to the total.", "Finder's Magic": "You can cast Hunter's Mark with this trait. At level 3 you can also cast Locate Object. Each spell works once, then returns after a Long Rest. Wisdom is your spellcasting ability for them."},
 "Air Genasi": {"Mingle with the Wind": "You can cast Levitate once with this trait, with no Material components, and regain it after a Long Rest. Constitution is your spellcasting ability for it."},
 "Earth Genasi": {"Merge with Stone": "You can cast Pass without Trace once with this trait, with no Material components, and regain it after a Long Rest. Constitution is your spellcasting ability for it."},
 "Fire Genasi": {"Reach to the Blaze": "You know the Produce Flame cantrip. At level 3 you can cast Burning Hands once as a level 1 spell and regain it after a Long Rest. Constitution is your spellcasting ability for these spells."},
 "Water Genasi": {"Call to the Wave": "You know the Shape Water cantrip. At level 3 you can cast Create or Destroy Water once as a level 2 spell and regain it after a Long Rest. Constitution is your spellcasting ability for these spells."},
 "Ghostwise Halfling": {"Silent Speech": "You can speak telepathically to a creature within 30 feet, one at a time. The creature understands you only if you share a language."},
 "Pallid Elf": {"Trance": "You do not sleep. You meditate semiconscious for 4 hours a day instead. The Common word for it is trance. While meditating you may dream after a fashion, which is really practiced mental exercise. You get the same benefit a human gets from 8 hours of sleep. A Long Rest taken this way ends after 4 hours. All other Long Rest rules still apply.", "Blessing of the Moon Weaver": "You know the Light cantrip. At level 3 you can cast Sleep once. At level 5 you can cast Invisibility once, on yourself only. Each returns after a Long Rest. None needs Material components. Wisdom is your spellcasting ability for them."},
 "Lotusden Halfling": {"Child of the Wood": "You know the Druidcraft cantrip. At level 3 you can cast Entangle once. At level 5 you can cast Spike Growth once. Each returns after a Long Rest. Neither needs Material components. Wisdom is your spellcasting ability for them.", "Timberwalk": "Checks to track you have Disadvantage. You cross Difficult Terrain made of nonmagical plants and undergrowth without spending extra movement."},
 "Draconblood Dragonborn": {"Breath Weapon": "As an action, you exhale destructive energy. Your draconic ancestry sets its size, shape and damage type, and the type of save. Each creature in the area makes that save against DC 8 plus your Constitution modifier plus your Proficiency Bonus. On a failure it takes 2d6 damage, and half on a success. The damage becomes 3d6 at level 6, 4d6 at level 11 and 5d6 at level 16. After using it, you need a Short or Long Rest to use it again.", "Forceful Presence": "You steer a conversation with clever diplomacy or menace. When you make a Charisma (Intimidation) or Charisma (Persuasion) check, you can make it with Advantage. You can do this once, then again after a Short or Long Rest."},
 "Ravenite Dragonborn": {"Breath Weapon": "As an action, you exhale destructive energy. Your draconic ancestry sets its size, shape and damage type, and the type of save. Each creature in the area makes that save against DC 8 plus your Constitution modifier plus your Proficiency Bonus. On a failure it takes 2d6 damage, and half on a success. The damage becomes 3d6 at level 6, 4d6 at level 11 and 5d6 at level 16. After using it, you need a Short or Long Rest to use it again.", "Vengeful Assault": "When a creature within range of a weapon you wield damages you, you can use your Reaction to attack it with that weapon. You can do this once, then again after a Short or Long Rest."},
 "Kaladesh Elf (Bishatar and Tirahar)": {"Trance": "You do not sleep. You meditate semiconscious for 4 hours a day instead. The Common word for it is trance. While meditating you may dream after a fashion, which is really practiced mental exercise. You get the same benefit a human gets from 8 hours of sleep. A Long Rest taken this way ends after 4 hours. All other Long Rest rules still apply."},
 "Kaladesh Elf (Vahadar)": {"Trance": "You do not sleep. You meditate semiconscious for 4 hours a day instead. The Common word for it is trance. While meditating you may dream after a fashion, which is really practiced mental exercise. You get the same benefit a human gets from 8 hours of sleep. A Long Rest taken this way ends after 4 hours. All other Long Rest rules still apply."},
 "Vedalken (Kaladesh)": {"Aether Lore": "On an Intelligence (History) check about magic items or aether-powered devices, you add twice your Proficiency Bonus instead of the normal amount."},
 "Aven (Dominaria)": {"Flight": "You have a Fly Speed of 30 feet. You cannot fly while you wear Medium or Heavy armor. If your campaign uses the encumbrance variant, you cannot fly while Encumbered.", "Hawkeyed": "You are proficient in Perception. Attacking at long range does not give you Disadvantage on ranged weapon attack rolls."},
 "Goblin (Grotag Tribe)": {"Grit": "You have Resistance to Fire and Psychic damage. While you wear no armor, your AC is 11 plus your Dexterity modifier."},
 "Goblin (Lavastep Tribe)": {"Grit": "You have Resistance to Fire and Psychic damage. While you wear no armor, your AC is 11 plus your Dexterity modifier."},
 "Goblin (Tuktuk Tribe)": {"Grit": "You have Resistance to Fire and Psychic damage. While you wear no armor, your AC is 11 plus your Dexterity modifier."},
 "Merfolk (Ula Creed)": {"Cantrip": "You know one cantrip from the Wizard spell list, your choice. Intelligence is your spellcasting ability for it."},
 "Dhampir (VRGR)": {"Ancestral Legacy": "When this lineage replaces a species, you may keep that species' skill proficiencies and its Climb, Fly or Swim Speed. If you keep none of them, or choose this lineage at character creation, you gain proficiency in two skills of your choice.", "Darkvision": "Within 60 feet, darkness looks like Dim Light to you, and Dim Light looks like Bright Light. Colors in that darkness show as gray tones.", "Vampiric Bite": "Your fanged bite is a natural weapon that counts as a simple melee weapon you are proficient with. You add your Constitution modifier, not Strength, to its attack and damage rolls. It deals 1d4 Piercing damage. While you are at half your Hit Points or fewer, you have Advantage on attack rolls with it. When it hits a creature that is not a Construct or Undead, you can choose one boost. Either regain Hit Points equal to the Piercing damage dealt, or gain a bonus to your next ability check or attack roll equal to that damage. You can use this a number of times equal to your Proficiency Bonus. All uses return after a Long Rest."},
 "Hexblood (VRGR)": {"Ancestral Legacy": "When this lineage replaces a species, you may keep that species' skill proficiencies and its Climb, Fly or Swim Speed. If you keep none of them, or choose this lineage at character creation, you gain proficiency in two skills of your choice.", "Darkvision": "Within 60 feet, darkness looks like Dim Light to you, and Dim Light looks like Bright Light. Colors in that darkness show as gray tones.", "Eerie Token": "As a Bonus Action, you painlessly remove a lock of hair, a nail or a tooth. The token holds magic until you finish a Long Rest. While it does, you can do the following. Telepathic Message: as an action, send a message of up to 25 words to the creature holding or carrying the token, if you are within 10 miles of it. Remote Viewing: if you are within 10 miles of the token, use an action to enter a trance for 1 minute. It ends early if you dismiss it (no action) or become Incapacitated. During it you see and hear from the token as if you were there, and you are Blinded and Deafened to your own surroundings. When the trance ends, the token is destroyed without harm. After you make a token, you cannot make another until you finish a Long Rest, when the missing part grows back.", "Hex Magic": "You can cast Disguise Self and Hex with this trait. Each works once this way, then returns after a Long Rest. You can also cast them with spell slots. Intelligence, Wisdom or Charisma is your spellcasting ability for them, chosen when you gain this lineage. Becoming a Hag: hags can perform a ritual that irreversibly turns a hexblood they created into a new hag, of their own kind or one matching the hexblood's nature. The hag and hexblood must be in the same place and both agree to the long ritual. Most hexbloods shun it, though some accept it over centuries. Afterward the hexblood is a hag NPC, no longer controlled by the player unless the DM rules otherwise."},
 "Reborn (VRGR)": {"Ancestral Legacy": "When this lineage replaces a species, you may keep that species' skill proficiencies and its Climb, Fly or Swim Speed. If you keep none of them, or choose this lineage at character creation, you gain proficiency in two skills of your choice.", "Deathless Nature": "You have escaped death, and gain these benefits. You have Advantage on saves against disease and the Poisoned condition, and Resistance to Poison damage. You have Advantage on death saving throws. You do not eat, drink or breathe. You do not sleep and magic cannot put you to sleep. A Long Rest takes you 4 hours if you spend them inactive and motionless but conscious.", "Knowledge from a Past Life": "You glimpse the past, maybe faded memories from ages ago or a former life. On a skill check, right after you see the d20, you can roll a d6 and add it to the total. You can do this a number of times equal to your Proficiency Bonus. All uses return after a Long Rest."},
 "Fairy (WBtW)": {"Flight": "Your wings give you a Fly Speed equal to your Speed. You cannot fly while you wear Medium or Heavy armor.", "Fairy Magic": "You know the Druidcraft cantrip. At level 3 you can cast Faerie Fire, and at level 5 Enlarge/Reduce. Each works once this way, then returns after a Long Rest. You can also cast them with spell slots of the right level. Intelligence, Wisdom or Charisma is your spellcasting ability for them, chosen when you pick this species."},
 "Harengon (WBtW)": {"Lucky Footwork": "After you fail a Dexterity save, you can spend your Reaction to roll a d4 and add it to that save. That can turn the failure into a success. You cannot do this while Prone or while your Speed is 0.", "Rabbit Hop": "As a Bonus Action, you can jump a distance equal to 5 times your Proficiency Bonus in feet, without provoking Opportunity Attacks. You need a Speed above 0 to do this. You can use it a number of times equal to your Proficiency Bonus. All uses return after a Long Rest."},
 "Tiefling (Devil's Tongue)": {"Devil's Tongue": "You know the Vicious Mockery cantrip. At level 3 you can cast Charm Person once as a level 2 spell. At level 5 you can cast Enthrall once. A Long Rest restores both. Charisma is your spellcasting ability for them. This trait replaces Infernal Legacy."},
 "Tiefling (Hellfire)": {"Hellfire": "You know the Thaumaturgy cantrip. At level 3 you can cast Burning Hands once a day as a level 2 spell. At level 5 you can also cast Darkness once a day. A Long Rest restores each. Charisma is your spellcasting ability for them."},
 "Tiefling (Winged)": {"Winged": "Bat-like wings grow from your shoulder blades. You have a Fly Speed of 30 feet while you do not wear Heavy armor."},
 "Zendikar Elf (Mul Daya)": {"Mul Daya Magic": "You know the Chill Touch cantrip. At level 3 you can cast Hex once, and at level 5 Darkness once. Each returns after a Long Rest. Wisdom is your spellcasting ability for them.", "Sunlight Sensitivity": "In direct sunlight, you have Disadvantage on attack rolls and on sight-based Wisdom (Perception) checks. This applies when you, your target or the thing you try to notice stands in the sun."},
 "Khoravar": {"Fey Gift": "You know the Friends cantrip. After each Long Rest you can swap it for another cantrip from the Cleric, Druid or Wizard list. Intelligence, Wisdom or Charisma is your spellcasting ability for it, chosen when you pick this species."},
 "Rimekin": {"Cold Fire Magic": "You know the Ray of Frost cantrip. At level 3 you learn Ice Knife, and at level 5 Flame Blade. You always have them prepared. Cast each once without a slot, regaining the use after a Long Rest. You can also cast them with spell slots. When you cast Flame Blade with this trait, it deals Cold damage instead of Fire. Intelligence, Wisdom or Charisma is your spellcasting ability for them, chosen when you pick this species."},
 "Dhampir": {"Darkvision": "Within 60 feet, darkness looks like Dim Light to you, and Dim Light looks like Bright Light.", "Spider Climb": "You have a Climb Speed equal to your Speed. At level 3 you can also move up, down and across vertical surfaces and along ceilings with your hands free.", "Trace of Undeath": "You have Resistance to Necrotic damage.", "Vampiric Bite": "When you deal damage with an Unarmed Strike, you can bite with your fangs instead. The bite deals Piercing damage equal to 1d4 plus your Constitution modifier in place of the normal damage. When it damages a creature that is not a Construct or Undead, you can choose one boost. Drain: regain Hit Points equal to the Piercing damage dealt. Strengthen: gain a bonus to the next ability check or attack roll you make within 1 minute, equal to the Piercing damage dealt. You can use this a number of times equal to your Proficiency Bonus. All uses return after a Long Rest."},
 "Hexblood": {"Darkvision": "Within 60 feet, darkness looks like Dim Light to you, and Dim Light looks like Bright Light.", "Eerie Token": "As a Bonus Action, you make a magic token by painlessly removing a lock of hair, a nail or something similar. While the token exists you gain these benefits. Distant Message: as a Magic action, send a telepathic message of 25 words or fewer to the creature holding or carrying the token, if you are within 10 miles of it. Remote Viewing: if you are within 10 miles of the token, use a Magic action to extend your senses through it for 1 minute. It ends early if you become Incapacitated or end it (no action). You see and hear from the token as if you were there. When it ends, the token is destroyed without harm. Otherwise the token lasts until you finish a Long Rest. After you make a token, you cannot make another until you finish a Long Rest.", "Hex Magic": "You always have Disguise Self and Hex prepared. You can cast each once without a spell slot, regaining that use after a Long Rest. You can also cast them with spell slots of the right level. Intelligence, Wisdom or Charisma is your spellcasting ability for them, chosen when you pick this species."},
 "Lupin": {"Feral Pounce": "Your Unarmed Strikes deal Slashing damage instead of Bludgeoning. When you hit with an Unarmed Strike as part of the Attack action on your turn, you can use both the damage and the Shove option. You can do this once per turn.", "Howl": "As a Bonus Action, you let out an eerie howl. Each creature you choose within 15 feet makes a Wisdom save (DC 8 plus your Constitution modifier plus your Proficiency Bonus) or has Disadvantage on attack rolls and saving throws until the start of your next turn. You can use it a number of times equal to your Proficiency Bonus. All uses return after a Long Rest."},
 "Reborn": {"Escaped Death": "You have Advantage on death saving throws.", "Everlasting": "Dehydration, malnutrition and suffocation do not give you Exhaustion. You do not sleep and magic cannot put you to sleep. A Long Rest takes you 4 hours if you spend them inactive and motionless but conscious.", "Knowledge from a Past Life": "You gain proficiency in one skill of your choice. You can also glimpse the past to help in the present. When you fail an ability check, you can roll 1d6 and add it to the d20, which may turn the failure into a success. You can do this a number of times equal to your Proficiency Bonus. All uses return after a Long Rest.", "Strange Endurance": "You have Resistance to one damage type of your choice: Cold, Necrotic or Poison."},
 "Orc (Ixalan)": {"Relentless Endurance": "When damage reduces you to 0 Hit Points without killing you outright, you drop to 1 Hit Point instead. You can do this once, then again after a Long Rest.", "Savage Attacks": "When you score a critical hit with a melee weapon attack, you roll one of the weapon's damage dice one extra time and add it to the critical hit's extra damage."},
 "Merfolk (Ixalan, Blue)": {"Cantrip": "You know one cantrip from the Wizard spell list, your choice. Intelligence is your spellcasting ability for it."},
 "Orc": {"Aggressive": "As a Bonus Action, you can move up to your Speed toward a hostile creature you can see or hear. You must end this move closer to it than you started.", "Primal Intuition": "Pick two of Animal Handling, Insight, Intimidation, Medicine, Nature, Perception and Survival. You are proficient in both."},
 "Dragonborn (Ravenite)": {"Languages": "You speak, read and write Common and Draconic. Draconic is among the oldest languages and is often used to study magic. Other creatures find it harsh, with many hard consonants and hissing sounds."},
 "Dhakaani Guul'dar (Bugbear)": {"Powerful Build": "You have Advantage on ability checks to end the Grappled condition. You count as one size larger for carrying capacity."},
 "Gnoll (Eberron)": {"Bite": "Your teeth are natural weapons for Unarmed Strikes. A hit deals Piercing damage equal to 1d6 plus your Strength modifier, instead of the usual Bludgeoning."},
 "Kalamer Landwalker (Merfolk)": {"Blessing of the Sea": "You have two forms: your natural merfolk form and a landwalker form. As a Magic action, you change from merfolk to landwalker. In landwalker form your tail splits into two legs, your Speed rises by 20 feet and your Swim Speed drops to 0. You stay in that form until you use a Magic action to return to merfolk form."},
 "Ruinbound": {"Personal Symbiont": "An alien being is part of your body and cannot be removed. It has a minor supernatural ability it can use for you. Choose one cantrip: Acid Splash, Guidance (self only), Light, Mage Hand, Mind Sliver, Poison Spray, Resistance (self only), Shocking Grasp or Thorn Whip. You can switch to another cantrip on the list after each Long Rest. Intelligence, Wisdom or Charisma is your spellcasting ability for it, chosen when you pick this species.", "Symbiont Mastery": "One magic item with the Symbiotic Nature property does not count against your attunement limit. After each Long Rest you can end your attunement to such an item, and you can ignore rules that would normally require Remove Curse or similar magic before the symbiont can be removed."},
 "Sahuagin": {"Blood Frenzy": "As a Bonus Action, you enter a blood frenzy until the start of your next turn. You have Advantage on attack rolls against any creature that is missing Hit Points. You can use this a number of times equal to your Proficiency Bonus. All uses return after a Long Rest.", "Natural Armor": "Your tough scales give you AC 12 plus your Dexterity modifier while you wear no armor. You can still use a Shield with it."},
 "Manikin": {"Born to Serve": "Manikins cannot hate or resent their makers, and that blind spot affects you in every situation. You have Disadvantage on Wisdom (Insight) checks.", "Integrated Gold Plating": "Your body has built-in protection. Worn armor gives you no AC, but a Shield works as normal. When you pick this species, choose a preset. Infiltrator: AC 11 plus your Dexterity modifier. You count as unarmored for features that need no armor. You have Advantage on Dexterity (Stealth) checks. Sentinel: AC 13 plus either your Dexterity modifier (maximum 2) or your Strength modifier (maximum 3). You count as wearing Medium armor, with its drawbacks if you lack training. Tormentor: AC 16 plus your Strength modifier (maximum 2). You count as wearing Heavy armor, with its drawbacks if you lack training. You have Disadvantage on Dexterity (Stealth) checks.", "Service Model": "You were built for a purpose. When you pick this species, choose one. Custodian: Advantage on ability checks to end the Grappled condition and one size larger for carrying capacity. When a creature within 5 feet is targeted by an attack, you can use your Reaction to jump in front. If it is willing, you swap places and become the target. You can do this a number of times equal to your Proficiency Bonus. All uses return after a Long Rest. Handler: proficiency in Stealth and with the Disguise Kit. You can carry up to two melee weapons with the Finesse or Light property inside your body. Over 1 hour, which can be done during a Short Rest, you can swap one or both for weapons you hold. You draw and stow them like normal weapons, and nobody can disarm you of them short of cutting off your arms. Thespian: proficiency in Performance. As a Bonus Action, you link yourself to a willing creature you see within 30 feet with immaterial strings for 1 hour.", "Age": "Manikins are not alive and do not age. Over time their materials lose their magic, and eventually they lose control of their whole body and return to being inanimate objects."},
 "Accursed (Steinhardt)": {"Blood Moon's Call": "If a Blood Moon is out while you sleep, you dream. In the dream the Black Goat plants a compulsion in your mind, a flood of images and sensations ordering you to do something. When you wake, you are affected by Suggestion (save DC 16) and must carry out the order. On a successful save you resist it.", "Legacy of a Thousand Young": "Your legacy gives you supernatural gifts. Pick one entry from the table and you gain its level 1 benefit. At levels 3 and 5 you learn a higher spell from the table. You always have it prepared, and can cast it once without a slot, regaining that use after a Long Rest. You can also cast it with spell slots. Intelligence, Wisdom or Charisma is your spellcasting ability for these spells, chosen when you pick the species."},
 "Cursed-Blood": {"Together Forever": "Your dead twin lives on inside your stomach in supernatural ways. Pick a Twin Manifestation from the table and gain its level 1 benefit. At levels 3 and 5 you gain more abilities. Any spell you learn from it is always prepared. You can cast it once without a slot, regaining that use after a Long Rest. You can also cast it with spell slots. Intelligence, Wisdom or Charisma is your spellcasting ability for these spells, chosen when you pick the twin.", "Age": "Cursed-bloods mature faster than humans, reaching adulthood at 5 years. They live shorter lives, usually about 60 years, and a rare few reach 100."},
 "Demidritch": {"Astral Sight": "You have Advantage on saves against the Blinded condition. You also know the Cosmic Eye cantrip, with Charisma as your spellcasting ability.", "Unleash Infinity": "From level 3, use a Bonus Action to transform for 1 minute. You pick the option when you select this species. You can end it early with no action. You can transform once, then again after a Long Rest. Nebulare: you burst with eldritch radiance. Creatures that are not your allies within 10 feet make a Dexterity save (DC 8 plus your Charisma modifier plus your Proficiency Bonus) or take d6s of Radiant damage equal to your Proficiency Bonus. You shed Bright Light in 10 feet and Dim Light 10 feet beyond, and a 20-foot Emanation around you is Difficult Terrain for enemies you see. At level 12 the Emanation grows to 60 feet, and you gain a Fly Speed of 30 feet and can hover. Oculare: while transformed, no creature within 60 feet gains Advantage on attack rolls against you because you are Invisible. At level 12 you also grow eye-covered wings, gaining a Fly Speed of 30 feet and Truesight out to 60 feet.", "Age": "Demidritchs mature faster than humans, reaching adulthood at 10 years. Their eldritch power wears their bodies down quickly, and they live up to about 60 years."},
 "Scourgeborne": {"Born of Madness": "Your body is not the one you were born with. It is the result of exposure to eldritch madness. Your option depends on your alignment. Good: your conviction lets you control dark impulses. You have Advantage on saves to avoid or end Madness, and Disadvantage on Charisma (Intimidation) checks. Neutral: no benefit or penalty. Evil: you let the depraved monster inside influence you. You gain a bonus to Dexterity saves equal to your Proficiency Bonus, and have Disadvantage on saves to avoid or end Madness.", "Eldritch Curse": "No mortal can undo your curse. You are immune to spells that change your form, such as Alter Self and Polymorph.", "Feral Limbs": "You can make Unarmed Strikes with horns, claws or fangs from your mutation. They deal Piercing damage equal to 1d6 plus your Strength modifier. If your alignment is Evil, your feral side dominates and the die becomes a d8.", "Monstrous Lineage": "You belong to a lineage with supernatural abilities. Pick one from the Monstrous Lineages table and gain its level 1 benefit. At levels 3 and 5 you gain further benefits listed on the table.", "Age": "Scourgeborne are made, not born. They mature at the same rate as the species they were before."},
 "Deep One Dwarf": {"Dreamer's Curse": "The dreams of the Slumbering One cloud your mind, and you struggle to tell reality from dreams. You have Disadvantage on ability checks and saves to see through illusions.", "Illusory Body": "Your body is covered in tentacles, clams, shells and other eldritch growths, but an illusion makes you look like an ordinary hill dwarf. Other Deep Ones see through it. You have Advantage on Charisma (Deception) checks against creatures that cannot see through the illusion.", "Otherworldly Resistance": "You have Resistance to Cold damage and Advantage on saves to avoid or end the Poisoned condition.", "Slumbering Legacy": "You descend from the Slumbering One and gain a supernatural boon. Choose one. Coiling Arm: your arm is a writhing mass of tentacles. Right after you hit with a melee attack on your turn, you can make an Unarmed Strike with the Grapple option against that enemy as a Bonus Action. Brutal Pincers: your forearm is a huge pincer used for Unarmed Strikes. A hit deals Piercing damage equal to 1d6 plus your Strength modifier, and the die is a d10 against a creature you are grappling. You cannot do fine work with this arm. Mucoid Extremities: your hands and feet are webbed and covered in suckers. Your Swim Speed becomes 35 feet and you gain a Climb Speed of 20 feet.", "Age": "Deep One Dwarves mature much faster than humans, reaching adulthood at 10. They cannot die of old age. Instead their minds slowly lose their grip on the body, and at about 50 years they become brainless servants of He Who Lies Dreaming."},
 "Amoeboid": {"Biomimicry": "As a Magic action, touch a creature and take its general shape. Your size and other statistics do not change. If it has a Burrow, Climb or Swim Speed, you gain that Speed, up to 40 feet. The change lasts 1 hour or until you dismiss it (no action). If your new form cannot wear clothing or armor, they fall off. Your ability to handle objects is set by the new form's limbs, not yours.", "Flexible Form": "As a Magic action, you stretch, squash and mold your body into a static shape of your choice, no more than 8 feet in any dimension. You copy the basic outline of something, but not its color, texture, moving parts or fine detail. You cannot change your volume. While in a static shape you cannot move, attack or cast spells."},
 "Avia-Ra": {"Avian Eyes": "You are proficient in Insight or Perception, your choice. You also have Advantage on saves to avoid or end the Blinded condition.", "Blessings of the Sun": "You know the Light cantrip. At level 3 you learn Bless, and at level 5 Lesser Restoration. You always have them prepared. You can cast each once without a slot, regaining that use after a Long Rest. You can also cast them with spell slots. Intelligence, Wisdom or Charisma is your spellcasting ability for them, chosen when you pick this species."},
 "Nautilid": {"Amphibious": "You can breathe underwater. Without your Aqua Suit, breathing air is uncomfortable, like a human in very thin air.", "Powerful Build": "You have Advantage on saves to end the Grappled condition. You count as one size larger for carrying capacity.", "Aqua Suit": "Unless you are aboard a whorl ship, you wear a bulky Aqua Suit filled with Poseidon Solution so you can breathe comfortably. It covers your whole body, with a large domed helmet you can see through. You can don or doff the helmet on your turn (no action) and the whole suit in 1 minute. A damaged suit that is not destroyed mends itself quickly, leaking little water. While wearing the suit you gain the benefits of the Armored Suit trait.", "Armored Suit": "Your AC is 13 plus your Dexterity modifier. You can wear armor over the Aqua Suit and use either armor for your AC. You can still use a Shield with it."},
 "Orc (Dark Matter)": {"Follow Through": "When you roll damage for a melee weapon attack or Unarmed Strike, you can reroll the damage dice and add 1d4 damage, and you must use the new roll. You can use this a number of times equal to your Proficiency Bonus. All uses return after a Long Rest.", "Gearhead": "Building wild vehicles is an orc pastime. You have Advantage on checks to repair or modify a ship. You are also proficient in Athletics, Intimidation, Piloting or Technology, your choice.", "Powerful Build": "You have Advantage on saves to end the Grappled condition. You count as one size larger for carrying capacity."},
 "Skathári": {"Adaptive Metabolism": "Your body adapts quickly to extreme settings. You take no damage from Extreme Temperatures in space and other harsh places (see the Glossary), and you can hold your breath for up to 15 minutes.", "Gliding Wings": "Membranous wings let you glide clumsily. While falling you can move up to 10 feet sideways for every 5 feet you fall, up to your Speed. You also have Resistance to falling damage.", "Hooked Claws": "Short claws tip your fingers. You can make Unarmed Strikes with them, using Dexterity instead of Strength for attack and damage rolls. They deal Slashing damage equal to 1d6 plus your Strength or Dexterity modifier.", "Natural Armor": "Your armored shell gives you AC 13 plus your Dexterity modifier while you wear no armor. You can still use a Shield with it.", "Regenerative": "If a body part is cut off but you survive, it regrows fully within 24 hours."},
 "Vect": {"Inflexible Mind": "Your mechanical mind is logical and hard to sway. You have Advantage on saves to avoid or end the Charmed condition on yourself.", "Construct Grafts": "As a Utilize action, you can install a Construct graft on your body. The first graft you install in a given body slot does not cost you Hit Points or Hit Point Dice. See the Equipment chapter for grafts. You start with Embedded Armor in the External slot: your AC is 13 plus your Dexterity modifier. You can wear armor over it and use either for your AC. You can still use a Shield with it."},
 "Wrothian": {"Psionic Blast": "When you take the Attack action, you can swap one attack for a burst of psionic energy at a creature you see within 60 feet. The target makes an Intelligence save (DC 8 plus your Constitution modifier plus your Proficiency Bonus). On a failure it takes 1d10 Psychic damage, and half on a success. The damage becomes 2d10 at level 5, 3d10 at level 11 and 4d10 at level 17. You can use it a number of times equal to your Proficiency Bonus. All uses return after a Long Rest.", "Razor Claws": "You can make Unarmed Strikes with teeth and claws, using Dexterity instead of Strength for attack and damage rolls. They deal Slashing damage equal to 1d6 plus your Strength or Dexterity modifier."},
 "Changeling (EFotA)": {"Shape-Shifter": "As an action, you change your looks and voice. You decide the details, such as coloring, hair length and sex. You can adjust height and weight and change your size between Medium and Small. You can look like a member of another playable species, but your game statistics stay the same. You cannot copy an individual you have never seen, and the form must have the same basic arrangement of limbs as yours. Clothing and gear do not change. While shape-shifted this way you have Advantage on Charisma checks. You stay in the form until you use an action to return to your true form."},
 "Shifter (EFotA)": {"Bestial Instincts": "Drawing on the beast within, you gain proficiency in one of Acrobatics, Athletics, Intimidation and Survival.", "Shifting": "As a Bonus Action, you take a more bestial form for 1 minute, ending early if you revert as a Bonus Action. Shifting gives you Temporary Hit Points equal to 2 times your Proficiency Bonus. You can shift a number of times equal to your Proficiency Bonus. All uses return after a Long Rest. Each time you shift you gain one option, chosen when you pick this species. Beasthide: 1d6 more Temporary Hit Points, and +1 AC while shifted. Longtooth: when you shift, and as a Bonus Action on later turns while shifted, make an Unarmed Strike with your long fangs. On a damaging hit it deals Piercing damage equal to 1d6 plus your Strength modifier instead of the normal damage. Swiftstride: while shifted your Speed rises by 10 feet, and you can use your Reaction to move up to 10 feet when a creature ends its turn within 5 feet of you, without provoking Opportunity Attacks. Wildhunt: while shifted you have Advantage on Wisdom checks, and no creature within 30 feet gains Advantage on attack rolls against you unless you are Incapacitated."},
 "Pluvenn": {"Razormaw": "Your teeth are natural weapons for Unarmed Strikes. A hit deals Piercing damage equal to 1d4 plus your Dexterity modifier, instead of the usual Bludgeoning."},
 "Manyhorn": {"Lifespan": "Manyhorn live 40 to 120 years and mature at 15 to 32, depending on the kind. Larger kinds usually live longer.", "Musclebound": "You count as one size larger for carrying capacity and for the weight you can push, drag or lift."},
 "Jeholrak": {"Lifespan": "Jeholrak usually live up to 50 years and mature at 16 or 17, mostly because their society is so competitive. Alone, a Jeholrak could reach the late 70s or early 80s.", "Power of Persuasion": "Jeholrak seem gifted at persuasion, but really they give off hypnotic pheromones that calm those around them. At certain levels you express this as spells. You can cast each once, at its lowest level, with no slot and no components, then you need a Long Rest to cast it again. The only sign is a faint sweet smell. Your spellcasting ability for them is Charisma, Constitution or Wisdom, your choice. They do not work on other Jeholrak. Level 1: Charm Person. Level 5: Suggestion.", "Cutthroat Society": "The Jeholrak of Mount Karao'kr will stab each other in the back, sometimes literally, to get ahead. You have Advantage on Sleight of Hand checks. As a Bonus Action you can hide a small one-handed weapon or item on yourself. Only a successful Investigation check or magic finds it. The DC is 8 plus your Dexterity modifier plus your Proficiency Bonus. It suits wands, daggers, hand axes, hand clubs and similar small items, but not something like a shortsword."},
 "Ankylier": {"Lifespan": "Ankylier live 110 to 130 years. They join Ankylier society at about 12 and are fully mature at about 25."},
 "Limukin": {"Lifespan": "Limukin mature fast and live short lives. They reach adult size at 6 to 8 years and full maturity at 9 or 10. They typically live to their early 20s, and the oldest elders are in their late 20s or early 30s.", "Envenomed Weapons": "As an action, you coat a weapon, or up to 5 pieces of ammunition, with venom you secrete. A creature damaged by it makes a Constitution save (DC 10 plus your Proficiency Bonus). On a failure it takes 2d4 Poison damage, which is 3d4 from level 5, and is Poisoned for 1 minute. The venom lasts 1 minute on the weapon before it dries and loses strength. You can do this once, then again after a Long Rest."},
 "Child of Seth": {"Flight": "You can fly with a Fly Speed of 30 feet, reduced to 20 feet while you wear Medium or Heavy armor.", "Verbal Culture": "Children of Seth have excellent memories and recall details from years ago fairly accurately. You are proficient in two of Arcana, Religion and History, or you can take Expertise in one of them instead. You remember every person you have met, including their name if you learned it."},
 "Cervan": {"Practical": "Cervans are practical and spend their time learning skills useful in woodland village life. You gain proficiency in one of Athletics, Medicine, Nature and Survival.", "Surge of Vigor": "Cervans are tenacious and bounce back from terrible blows. If one attack deals damage greater than half your current Hit Points, even if it drops you to 0, you immediately regain Hit Points equal to 1d12 plus your Constitution modifier. You can do this once, then again after a Long Rest."},
 "Corvum": {"Glide": "With your feathered arms, you can slow a fall and glide short distances. When you fall, use your Reaction to spread your arms and stiffen your wing feathers. You then descend gently at 60 feet per round and take no fall damage on landing. If the fall is at least 10 feet, you can instead move horizontally up to your Speed in a direction you choose, though not upward, and land where the move ends. You cannot glide while carrying heavy weapons, holding a shield, wearing Heavy armor or Encumbered. You may drop held items as part of the Reaction to free your arms.", "Talons": "Your sharp claws help in unarmed fights and in climbing. Your Unarmed Strike deals 1d4 Piercing damage. You also have Advantage on Strength (Athletics) checks to climb any surface your talons could grip.", "Appraising Eye": "You have an uncanny talent for judging objects. By spending an action studying an object, you learn its magical properties, how to use or activate them, and a fair estimate of its market price. It strains your eyes, so you must finish a Short or Long Rest before using it again."},
 "Gallus": {"Glide": "With your feathered arms, you can slow a fall and glide short distances. When you fall, use your Reaction to spread your arms and stiffen your wing feathers. You then descend gently at 60 feet per round and take no fall damage on landing. If the fall is at least 10 feet, you can instead move horizontally up to your Speed in a direction you choose, though not upward, and land where the move ends. You cannot glide while carrying heavy weapons, holding a shield, wearing Heavy armor or Encumbered. You may drop held items as part of the Reaction to free your arms.", "Wing Flap": "As a Bonus Action, you beat your strong feathered arms and rise a distance equal to half your Speed. You can combine it with a normal jump, but not while gliding.", "Communal": "On an Intelligence (History) check about the history of your species, culture or community, you count as proficient in History and add twice your Proficiency Bonus instead of the normal amount.", "Of the People": "Pick one set of artisan's tools: brewer's supplies, carpenter's tools or smith's tools. You are proficient with it."},
 "Hedge": {"Natural Burrowers": "You have a Burrow Speed of 15 feet. You can dig through soil, but with only your clawed hands you cannot dig through anything tougher.", "Spiny Quills": "Spiny quills cover your back, so you cannot wear armor. They protect you well, giving you AC 14 plus your Dexterity modifier. You can still use a Shield you are proficient with.", "Curl Up": "As an action, you curl into a ball and show attackers a wall of tough quills. While curled you cannot move, attack or cast spells with somatic components, and your AC is 19. You lose your Dexterity bonus to AC while curled, but a Shield still works. A creature that misses you with a melee attack while you are curled takes 2d4 Piercing damage from your quills. If a creature hits you while you are curled, you are knocked Prone in your space at the end of the turn. You can uncurl at any point on your turn.", "Forest Magic": "You are deeply tied to the magic of the Wood. You know the Druidcraft cantrip. You can also cast Animal Messenger once as a level 2 spell and regain it after a Short or Long Rest. Charisma is your spellcasting ability for these spells.", "Speak With Bugs": "With sounds and gestures, you can pass simple ideas to Beasts that are insects, spiders, worms and similar crawling creatures, of any size."},
 "Luma": {"Glide": "With your feathered arms, you can slow a fall and glide short distances. When you fall, use your Reaction to spread your arms and stiffen your wing feathers. You then descend gently at 60 feet per round and take no fall damage on landing. If the fall is at least 10 feet, you can instead move horizontally up to your Speed in a direction you choose, though not upward, and land where the move ends. You cannot glide while carrying heavy weapons, holding a shield, wearing Heavy armor or Encumbered. You may drop held items as part of the Reaction to free your arms.", "Wing Flap": "As a Bonus Action, you beat your strong feathered arms and rise a distance equal to half your Speed. You can combine it with a normal jump, but not while gliding.", "Fated": "Luck or a guiding presence always seems to put you on the right path. After you roll an attack, skill check or saving throw, and before the outcome is decided, you can reroll it. You can do this once, then again after a Long Rest."},
 "Mapach": {"Darkvision": "Within 60 feet, darkness looks like Dim Light to you, and Dim Light looks like Bright Light. You see no color in the dark, only gray tones. Mapachs are most at ease at night.", "Scroungecraft": "You are proficient with tinker's tools. You can also build rough but working versions of common items from materials around you. Spend 10 minutes to make any tool or piece of adventuring gear worth 30 gp or less. It works fully and can even pass as a disguise if you made clothing. Tools and anything that would break on first use, such as caltrops or arrows, become useless afterward. Otherwise the item lasts 1 hour before it falls apart. The GM can rule that the right materials are not at hand. For example, a vial of acid is easy near a nest of acidic beetle larvae, and bark can be bound into a flask, but passable silk robes from a pile of leaves are not. If you have the right materials, you can spend 8 hours turning a scroungecrafted item into a permanent one, if you start before it falls apart. It then works like a normal one, and if you are proficient with the tools used it can look professionally made. Otherwise it looks rough and cobbled together. You can also repair broken equipment this way if you have materials, though the GM decides how long the repair lasts."},
 "Raptor": {"Glide": "With your feathered arms, you can slow a fall and glide short distances. When you fall, use your Reaction to spread your arms and stiffen your wing feathers. You then descend gently at 60 feet per round and take no fall damage on landing. If the fall is at least 10 feet, you can instead move horizontally up to your Speed in a direction you choose, though not upward, and land where the move ends. You cannot glide while carrying heavy weapons, holding a shield, wearing Heavy armor or Encumbered. You may drop held items as part of the Reaction to free your arms.", "Talons": "Your sharp claws help in unarmed fights and in climbing. Your Unarmed Strike deals 1d4 Piercing damage. You also have Advantage on Strength (Athletics) checks to climb any surface your talons could grip.", "Hunter's Training": "You are proficient with the longbow, shortbow and spear. The longbow does not count as a heavy weapon for you."},
 "Strig": {"Glide": "With your feathered arms, you can slow a fall and glide short distances. When you fall, use your Reaction to spread your arms and stiffen your wing feathers. You then descend gently at 60 feet per round and take no fall damage on landing. If the fall is at least 10 feet, you can instead move horizontally up to your Speed in a direction you choose, though not upward, and land where the move ends. You cannot glide while carrying heavy weapons, holding a shield, wearing Heavy armor or Encumbered. You may drop held items as part of the Reaction to free your arms.", "Talons": "Your sharp claws help in unarmed fights and in climbing. Your Unarmed Strike deals 1d4 Piercing damage. You also have Advantage on Strength (Athletics) checks to climb any surface your talons could grip.", "Darkvision": "You hunt well under the cover of night. Within 60 feet, darkness looks like Dim Light to you, and Dim Light looks like Bright Light. You see no color in the dark, only gray tones."},
 "Vulpin": {"Darkvision": "You hunt well at night. Within 60 feet, darkness looks like Dim Light to you, and Dim Light looks like Bright Light. You see no color in the dark, only gray tones.", "Bite": "Your sharp fangs let you bite as an Unarmed Strike dealing 1d6 Piercing damage. You can use Strength or Dexterity for both the attack roll and the damage.", "Bewitching Guile": "You can cast Charm Person once as a level 1 spell and regain it after a Long Rest. At level 3 you can cast Ambush Prey once as a level 2 spell, and at level 5 Fear once. Each returns after a Long Rest. Intelligence is your spellcasting ability for them."}
};
export const MORE14_BACKGROUND = {
 "Anthropologist|Adept Linguist": "You can talk with Humanoids who share no language with you. First watch them interact with one another for at least 1 day. You then learn a few key words, phrases and gestures, enough for basic communication.",
 "Archaeologist|Historical Knowledge": "When you enter a ruin or dungeon, you can tell what it was originally for and who built it, whether dwarves, elves, humans, yuan-ti or another known people. You can also work out the monetary value of art objects more than a century old.",
 "Astral Drifter|Divine Contact": "You gain the Magic Initiate feat from the Player's Handbook and must choose Cleric for it. In the Astral Sea you briefly met a wandering deity in a peaceful encounter. The deity shared one secret or scrap of cosmic lore with you. Work out with your DM what it is and how it affects the campaign. Roll on the Divine Contact table to find the deity, or agree on a better fit with your DM.",
 "Wildspacer|Wildspace Adaptation": "You gain the Tough feat from the Player's Handbook. You also know how to cope with zero gravity. Being weightless never gives you Disadvantage on melee attack rolls.",
 "Athlete|Echoes of Victory": "Spectators, other athletes and trainers near the place of your past wins admire you. In any settlement within 100 miles of where you grew up, you have a 50 percent chance of finding a fan who will share information or put you up for a while. Between adventures you can enter athletic contests to fund a Comfortable lifestyle, using the Practicing a Profession downtime activity in chapter 8 of the Player's Handbook.",
 "Grinner|Ballad of the Grinning Fool": "Grinners always know where to hole up. In a city of 10,000 or more people on the Menagerie Coast or in the Dwendalian Empire, perform the Ballad of the Grinning Fool at a major tavern or inn. The Golden Grin finds you and houses you and any companions you vouch for. The DM can end this if hiding you becomes too risky. Be careful with it. Some people who know the ballad are traitors, counterspies or agents of tyranny.",
 "Volstrucker Agent|Shadow Network": "You can reach other Volstrucker over long distances. Write a letter in special arcane ink, address it to a member of the order and burn it in a fire. The ash turns back into the whole letter on the person of the agent you named. The ink is the same ink a wizard uses to scribe spells. A page costs 10 gp.",
 "Azorius Functionary|Legal Authority": "You enforce Ravnica's laws, and that rank earns you respect and some fear. Folk behave around you, steer clear of your notice and assume you may go where you please. Showing your Azorius insignia gets you a meeting with anyone you wish to speak to, although it can backfire on hardened lawbreakers. Abuse it and your superiors can discipline you or take your position.",
 "Boros Legionnaire|Legion Station": "You hold an established place in the Boros Legion hierarchy. You can requisition simple equipment for temporary use, and enter any Boros garrison in Ravnica to rest safely and receive medics. You are paid 1 gp a week (a Boros-minted 1-zino coin). With free lodging in the garrison, that supports a Poor lifestyle between adventures.",
 "Dimir Operative|False Identity": "You live under more than one identity. The one you wear most shows you as a member of a guild other than House Dimir. You have papers, known acquaintances and disguises to pass as that persona and fit into the second guild. You can drop it at will and blend into the guildless crowd. Decide with your DM why you are embedded in that guild, using the table or a reason you like.",
 "Golgari Agent|Undercity Paths": "You know hidden underground routes that bypass crowds, obstacles and watchers. When not in combat, you and companions you lead travel between any two places in the city at twice the speed you normally could. The undercity holds dangers that seldom reach the surface, so the trip is not guaranteed safe.",
 "Gruul Anarch|Rubblebelt Refuge": "You know the places most people avoid: ruined districts flattened by wurms, parks left overgrown for decades, and wide rubblebelts of broken ground. In such areas you can find a good spot for you and your allies to hide or rest. You can also find food and fresh water there each day for yourself and up to five other people.",
 "Izzet Engineer|Urban Infrastructure": "The Izzet League is known for mad inventions and explosions. It also does ordinary construction work, building the infrastructure behind running water, levitating platforms and other wonders. You understand how buildings are put together, including what is behind the walls. You can find blueprints of a specific building and learn how it was built, such as entry points, structural weak spots or hidden spaces. Your access is limited. If using the information causes legal trouble, the guild will not shield you.",
 "Orzhov Representative|Leverage": "You hold sway over people beneath you in the guild and can demand help from them when needed. Examples: carrying a message across a neighborhood, a free short carriage ride, or cleaning up a bloody mess you left in an alley. The DM rules whether a demand is fair and whether any subordinate is on hand. As your rank grows, you hold sway over more people, including more powerful ones.",
 "Rakdos Cultist|Fearsome Reputation": "People know you as a member of the Cult of Rakdos and take care not to provoke your anger or mockery. You can get away with minor crimes, such as refusing to pay for a meal or breaking a shop door, if no legal authority sees it. Most people are too intimidated to report you to the Azorius.",
 "Selesnya Initiate|Conclave's Shelter": "Your fellow Selesnya Conclave members will shelter and assist you. In any Selesnya enclave in the city, you and your companions can hide or rest, unless you have shown yourself dangerous to it. Enclave members hide you from the law and from anyone hunting you, but will not put their lives at stake. You can also get free healing at an enclave. You supply any Material components yourself.",
 "Simic Scientist|Researcher": "When you try to learn or recall a magical or scientific fact you do not know, you know where and from whom to get it. Usually that is a Simic laboratory, sometimes an Izzet facility, a library, a university, or an independent scholar. Knowing where it is does not mean you get it. You may need bribes, favors or other incentives. Your DM may rule that the knowledge is hidden somewhere unreachable or cannot be found. The deepest secrets of the multiverse can take an adventure or a whole campaign.",
 "Faceless|Dual Personalities": "Your allies and the world know you by your persona. Anyone digging into your weaknesses, origins or purpose is stopped by your disguise. When you wear a disguise and act as your persona, nobody can tell you are your true self. When you remove it and show your real face, nobody can tell you are your persona. You can switch between the two as often as you like, using one to hide the other. If someone figures out the link between them, the deception may stop working.",
 "Celebrity Adventurer's Scion|Name Dropping": "You know many powerful people across the land, and some may remember you. At the DM's discretion, you can get minor help from a major figure in the campaign. Common folk treat you with deference, and your heritage and stories can earn a free meal or a bed.",
 "Failed Merchant|Supply Chain": "From your time as a merchant you keep ties with wholesalers, suppliers and other traders and entrepreneurs. You can use these connections when you look for items or information.",
 "Plaintiff|Legalese": "Your time in the local legal system taught you how it works. Even when the law is against you, you can use dense terms such as ex injuria jus non oritur and cogitationis poenam nemo patitur to make people think you know what you are talking about. With ordinary folk who know no better, you may be able to intimidate or deceive them into favors or special treatment.",
 "Rival Intern|Inside Informant": "You keep ties with your former employer or with other groups you dealt with at work. You can contact them and gain information, as the DM decides.",
 "City Watch|Watcher's Eye": "Years of policing give you a sense for local laws and criminals. You quickly find the nearest watch post or similar body, and just as quickly identify the criminal haunts of a community. The first will usually welcome you far more than the second.",
 "Variant City Watch (Investigator)|Watcher's Eye": "Years of policing give you a sense for local laws and criminals. You quickly find the nearest watch post or similar body, and just as quickly identify the criminal haunts of a community. The first will usually welcome you far more than the second.",
 "Clan Crafter|Respect of the Stout Folk": "Outsiders respect clan crafters, but dwarves respect them most. You always get free room and board wherever shield dwarves or gold dwarves live. Residents may compete to offer you, and possibly your companions, the best lodging and help.",
 "Cloistered Scholar|Library Access": "Others often face long interviews and high fees to enter even the common archives of your library. You get free, easy access to most of it, though some collections are too valuable, magical or secret to open to anyone at once. You know your cloister's staff and bureaucracy and how to work with them. You are also likely to get preferential treatment at other libraries across the Realms, as a courtesy to a fellow scholar.",
 "Courtier|Court Functionary": "You understand how bureaucracies run, so you can reach the records and inner workings of any noble court or government you meet. You know who holds the real power, who to ask for a favor, and which intrigues are currently drawing attention.",
 "Faction Agent|Safe Haven": "You can draw on a secret network of supporters and operatives who help you on adventures. You know secret signs and passwords that mark them. They can provide a hidden safe house, free room and board, or help finding information. They will not risk their lives for you or reveal who they are.",
 "Far Traveler|All Eyes on You": "Your accent, manners, turns of phrase and perhaps your looks mark you as foreign. People glance at you wherever you go, which can annoy you, but scholars and others curious about distant lands take a friendly interest, as do ordinary people who want stories of your home. You can turn that attention into access to people and places that would otherwise be closed to you and your companions. Nobles, scholars and merchant princes may want to hear about your homeland and its people.",
 "Inheritor|Inheritance": "Pick or roll your inheritance on the table. Fill in the details with your DM, such as why it matters and its whole history. You can also leave the DM to invent details during play, so you learn about it as your character does. The DM may turn it into a story hook, sending you to learn its past or true nature, or pitting you against foes who want it or want you kept ignorant. The DM also sets its properties.",
 "Knight of the Order|Knightly Regard": "Members of your knightly order, and people who share its aims, give you shelter and help. A religious order's knights can get aid from temples and communities of their deity. Knights of civic orders get help from the community they serve, whether one settlement or a nation. Knights of philosophical orders get help from those they aided and from people who share their ideals. Help means shelter, meals and healing when fitting, and sometimes risky aid, such as locals rallying to your side.",
 "Mercenary Veteran|Mercenary Life": "You know mercenary life from the inside. You can identify mercenary companies by their emblems and know a little about each, including who hired them lately. In any area you can find the taverns and festhalls where mercenaries gather, if you speak the language. Between adventures you can find mercenary work enough to keep a comfortable lifestyle, using Practicing a Profession in chapter 8 of the Player's Handbook.",
 "Urban Bounty Hunter|Ear to the Ground": "You are in regular contact with the people your quarries move among. They may belong to the criminal underworld, the street crowd or high society. In any city you visit you have a contact who gives you information about the local people and places.",
 "Uthgardt Tribe Member|Uthgardt Heritage": "You know your tribe's lands well, and also the terrain and natural resources of the rest of the North. In any wilderness you know well enough to find twice as much food and water as normal when you forage. You can also ask for the hospitality of your people and their allies, often including druid circles, nomadic elf tribes, the Harpers and priesthoods of the First Circle gods.",
 "Waterdhavian Noble|Kept in Style": "In Waterdeep or elsewhere in the North, your house covers your daily needs. Your name and signet cover most costs. Inns, taverns and festhalls log your debt and send the bill to your family's estate in Waterdeep. This lets you live a Comfortable lifestyle without paying 2 gp a day, or cuts that amount from a Wealthy or Aristocratic lifestyle. You cannot live more cheaply and keep the difference. It is a line of credit, not money.",
 "Feylost|Feywild Connection": "Natives of the Feywild recognize your manners and knowledge of fey customs and see you as one of their own. Friendly Fey creatures tend to help you if you are lost or need aid in the Feywild.",
 "Marine|Steady": "You can travel twice as long each day, up to 16 hours, before a forced march affects you (see Travel Pace in chapter 8 of the Player's Handbook). You also automatically find a safe way to land a boat on shore, if one exists.",
 "Shipwright|I'll Patch It!": "With carpenter's tools and wood, you can repair a water vehicle. You restore Hit Points to its hull equal to 5 times your Proficiency Bonus. You cannot patch the same vehicle again until it has been hauled ashore and fully repaired.",
 "Smuggler|Down Low": "You know a network of smugglers who help you out of tight spots. In a given town, city or similar community (DM's call), you and your companions can stay free in safe houses. They offer a Poor lifestyle. While there, you can choose to keep your presence, and your companions', secret.",
 "Gate Warden|Planar Infusion": "A childhood in a gate-town or similar place left you steeped in planar energy. You gain the Scion of the Outer Planes feat. You also know where to get free, simple lodging and food in the community where you grew up.",
 "Haunted One|Heart of Darkness": "People can see in your eyes that you have faced terrible horror and know darkness well. They may fear you, yet commoners show you every courtesy and do their best to help. Unless you have shown yourself a danger to them, they will even take up arms and fight beside you if you face an enemy alone.",
 "Investigator|Official Inquiry": "You are skilled at getting access to people and places for information. With fast talk, persistence and official-looking papers, you can reach a place or person tied to a crime you are investigating. People not involved in the case do not get in your way, or pass your requests along. Local law enforcement has firm opinions of you, seeing you as a nuisance or one of their own.",
 "House Agent|House Connections": "As your house's agent, you can always get meals and a bed for yourself and friends at a house enclave. A mission from the house normally comes with the supplies and transport you need. You also have old friends, mentors and rivals in the house, and may run into one during house business. How willing they are to help depends on where you stand in the house.",
 "Initiate|Trials of the Five Gods": "Your life centers on the five trials that decide your worth in the afterlife. While you prepare for and take the trials, you always have access to training. Servitor mummies (the anointed), supervised by viziers, give you a comfortable home and regular meals. You keep these benefits only while you follow Naktamun's norms: training for the trials, obeying the gods' orders and following the viziers' instructions. If you break these norms, you risk being treated as a dissenter.",
 "Vizier|Voice of Authority": "Your voice is your god's voice, at least in theory. Your work may include training and instructing initiates, who must obey you. An initiate is expected to defer to you and follow your commands. If you abuse this authority, your god may punish you personally.",
 "Inquisitor|Legal Authority": "As an inquisitor of the church you can arrest criminals. When no other authority is present, you may pass judgment and carry out sentences. If you abuse this power, your church superiors may take it from you.",
 "Lorehold Student|Lorehold Initiate": "You gain the Strixhaven Initiate feat, choosing Lorehold. If you have Spellcasting or Pact Magic, the Lorehold Spells table is added to your class spell list (every list, if you multiclass). Think about how your spells look. Golden light, a tome or scroll as your focus, and effects that echo the reference books you study all suit Lorehold.",
 "Prismari Student|Prismari Initiate": "You gain the Strixhaven Initiate feat, choosing Prismari. If you have Spellcasting or Pact Magic, the Prismari Spells table is added to your class spell list (every list, if you multiclass). Think about how your spells look. Casting can be sweeping, dance-like motion, with each blast shaped like a work of art and elemental forces forming grand patterns.",
 "Quandrix Student|Quandrix Initiate": "You gain the Strixhaven Initiate feat, choosing Quandrix. If you have Spellcasting or Pact Magic, the Quandrix Spells table is added to your class spell list (every list, if you multiclass). Think about how your spells look. Spells might unfold in swirling fractal patterns, and magic that makes or alters creatures might wrap them in shimmering fractals.",
 "Silverquill Student|Silverquill Initiate": "You gain the Strixhaven Initiate feat, choosing Silverquill. If you have Spellcasting or Pact Magic, the Silverquill Spells table is added to your class spell list (every list, if you multiclass). Think about how your spells look. Ink splashes or ripples of golden light suit Silverquill, and verbal components may sound like loud echoes of your own voice.",
 "Witherbloom Student|Witherbloom Initiate": "You gain the Strixhaven Initiate feat, choosing Witherbloom. If you have Spellcasting or Pact Magic, the Witherbloom Spells table is added to your class spell list (every list, if you multiclass). Think about how your spells look. Components or a focus from the Witherbloom swamp fit well, and your magic might look like those plants and creatures, perhaps with ghostly swamp animals.",
 "Rewarded|Fortune's Favor": "Sudden good fortune shows as a small boon. You gain one feat of your choice: Lucky, Magic Initiate or Skilled. The feat mirrors what changed your life. A genie's three wishes might become Magic Initiate. A lucky game of three-dragon ante that cleared your family's debts might be Lucky. Skilled can reflect a trial you survived and the know-how it gave you.",
 "Ruined|Still Standing": "You survived ruinous misfortune and hold hidden reserves others do not expect. You gain the Alert, Skilled or Tough feat, your choice. It reflects how you handled the loss that changed your life. If you kept your senses sharp and climbed out of misery by seizing small chances, choose Alert. If you redoubled your effort to win back what was yours, choose Skilled. If you endured calmly through the hardship, choose Tough.",
 "Bandit Defector|Bandit Routes": "You helped with many highway robberies, so you know the roads of the Wood and the escape routes bandits use. When not in combat, you and companions you lead travel between places through forested land at twice the normal speed.",
 "Grounded|Find Another Path": "You have lived close to the ground and know the undergrowth as other birdfolk know the canopy. You always recall the general layout of the terrain while traveling on the forest floor. If an obstacle that needs climbing or gaining height blocks your path, you can always find a way around it, as long as one exists. You are also good at finding shelter in the Wood and can usually find a safe spot, such as a cave, tree hollow or bramble thicket, on the forest floor for you and up to five other creatures.",
 "Wind-Touched|Supernatural Presence": "Whether or not you are truly Wind-Touched, people across Everden believe the wind has blessed you. When you show power or skill that can be credited to wind or air, such as acrobatic feats or commanding unseen forces, believers are encouraged by your presence. They support you and, depending on how well you convince them, treat you with reverence or even worship.",
 "Fisher|Harvest the Water": "You have Advantage on ability checks made with fishing tackle. If you can reach a body of water that supports sea life, you can keep a Moderate lifestyle by working as a fisher. Each day you can catch enough food to feed yourself and up to ten other people.",
 "Gambler|Never Tell Me the Odds": "Odds and probability are your trade. During downtime activities that involve games of chance, or judging the odds of the best plan, you get a solid sense of which choice is probably best and which opportunities look too good to be true. The DM decides what you learn.",
 "Planar Philosopher|Conviction": "You gain the Scion of the Outer Planes feat. Members of your organization also give you free, modest lodging and food at any of their holdings or in the homes of other faction members.",
 "Witchlight Hand|Carnival Fixture": "The Witchlight Carnival gives you free, modest lodging and food. You can also wander the carnival and enjoy its wonders at no cost, as long as you do not disrupt its shows or cause other trouble.",
 "Archivist|Expert Researcher": "You know your way around libraries and the books in them. Spend 1 hour studying the books, scrolls and records of an archive or similar collection while you research one subject. After that, any Intelligence check about that subject that totals 9 or lower counts as a 10. You cannot use this again on the same subject in the same library.",
 "Ashari|Elemental Harmony": "You grew up near wild elemental magic and learned to nudge it a little. As an action, use a tiny effect tied to the element of your Ashari order. Pyrah: make a burst of flame big enough to light a candle, torch or small campfire, or put out a flame of that size. Terrah: make a small stone no bigger than a gold piece in your hand. It crumbles to dust after 1 minute. Vesrah: make enough hot or cold water to fill a small cup. Zephrah: make a puff of wind strong enough to scatter papers on a desk or ruffle hair. Each effect is instant.",
 "Clasp Member|A Favor in Turn": "Once you rank high enough in the Clasp, you can call in a favor from your contacts whenever you are near a place where the syndicate is active. State the request in 20 words or fewer. It goes up the chain to an unnamed Spireling, who approves or refuses it. The GM decides how an approved favor plays out. If you ask for muscle, a Clasp member can help your party for a time. If you ask for money, you get a small loan. If you are jailed, operatives may try to free you or bribe the jailer. Every favor leaves a debt. The Clasp can collect it at any moment and often gives no warning. Refuse and you die. A payment might be a burglary, squeezing an Emonian official for a secret before a ball, or even a killing with no questions allowed. The GM should keep the price in proportion to the favor, or reward you in other ways when the job goes beyond simple repayment.",
 "Clasp Member|Variant: Myriad Operative": "Your standing in the Myriad lets you call in a favor from your contacts when you are near a hub of Myriad activity. Keep the request to 20 words or fewer. It goes up the chain to an unnamed Spireling for approval. The GM shapes how the favor is met and how fast. Muscle means a Myriad minion helps the party for a time. Money means a small loan. If you are locked up, they may try to free you or pay off the jailer. Each favor creates a debt that can be called without notice and often at once. You might be sent on a burglary, told to press an official for a secret at a ball, or ordered to kill an NPC with no questions asked. The GM sets a repayment that fits the favor. If you leave a debt unpaid for too long, you lose your place in the Myriad, and a large debt can make you the next target on a contract. The Myriad also deals in slaves and rare goods, so you sometimes get servants or rare materials without using the favor. Your contacts are far fewer, and if a Clasp member exposes you as Myriad, they will hunt you.",
 "Fey Hostage|First Impressions": "You grew up in a fey court where one wrong courtesy could ruin you. First impressions shaped everything, and you still act on that. The first time you deal with a given fey creature, you gain a +2 bonus on the first Charisma check you make against it.",
 "Lyceum Scholar|Academic Requisition": "You have passed enough courses, and made a friend or two among the staff, to enter private areas of the Lyceum and allied universities. At the Lyceum or any large academic institution, you may borrow any tool set from the core rules. A magical tag on each set sounds an alarm when it leaves the campus. When you pay an NPC at the Alabaster Lyceum or a related school for services such as spellcasting, you can get a 25 percent discount, at the GM's discretion.",
 "Lyceum Student|Student Privilege": "Your coursework and a friend or two on staff give you entry to chambers at the Lyceum, and at some allied universities, that outsiders cannot enter. You can use any tool kit there, as long as you use it on the grounds and leave it in its chamber. Each kit is magically tagged and sounds an alarm if removed. Risky kits, such as a poisoner's kit, and advanced work, such as enchanting an item, may need staff supervision. You may also get cheap crafting materials and enchanting tables for free if the cost is low or your argument is good. Staff approval and the DM's call decide.",
 "Myriad Operative|A Favor in Turn": "Your standing in the Myriad lets you call in a favor from your contacts when you are near a hub of Myriad activity. Keep the request to 20 words or fewer. It goes up the chain to an unnamed Spireling for approval. The GM shapes how the favor is met and how fast. Muscle means a Myriad minion helps the party for a time. Money means a small loan. If you are locked up, they may try to free you or pay off the jailer. Each favor creates a debt that can be called without notice and often at once. You might be sent on a burglary, told to press an official for a secret at a ball, or ordered to kill an NPC with no questions asked. The GM sets a repayment that fits the favor. If you leave a debt unpaid for too long, you lose your place in the Myriad, and a large debt can make you the next target on a contract.",
 "Myriad Operative|Slavery and Exotic Trade": "The Myriad trades in slaves and rare goods. At times you gain servants or rare materials through the group without spending a favor. Your contacts are far fewer than a Clasp member's, and if a Clasp member exposes you as Myriad, you will be hunted.",
 "Recovered Cultist|Wicked Awareness": "Your years of secret worship taught you to notice others who still follow dark powers. You often spot hidden signs, messages and signals left in settled places. When you actively look for traces of a cult or dark following, you find and read the signs, or the social cues of cult activity, with ease. You have advantage on ability checks to uncover such details.",
 "Reformed Cultist|Fell Teachings": "The cult drilled you in the faith of one Betrayer God, from its basic commandments to its deepest secrets. Choose one Betrayer God. You roll with both advantage and disadvantage on Intelligence (Religion) checks to recall facts about that faith, including obscure secrets most worshipers never learn. You can also work with your GM to invent a secret you learned in the cult. It might seed a conspiracy, twist a legend of a hero whose meaning has drifted, or point to a lost artifact of the gods.",
 "Soul Channeler|Channel Essence": "You draw on your inner reserves to heal lasting injuries. During a short rest, you can cancel a reduction to one of your ability scores by spending Hit Dice equal to twice the reduction you want to remove. These Hit Dice give no other benefit, such as extra hit points.",
 "Transformed Familiar|Familiar Insight": "Your body has changed, but you keep the instincts you were born with. When you deal with creatures of your original type, you gain a +2 bonus on Wisdom (Insight) checks to read their motives and judge whether they tell the truth.",
 "Whitestone Rifle Corps|Legacy of Secrecy": "You were trusted with a powerful and frightening weapon made by Percival de Rolo. It may change war and life in Tal'Dorei, and it weighs on its maker's mind. Your commander in the Whitestone Rifle Corps gave you a musket or a pistol. It marks your standing. When you show it, people nearby treat you differently, especially adventurers, mercenaries, guards, engineers and weapon fans. They may see you as a noble defender, a greedy hoarder of power, or anything between. The GM decides.",
 "Whitestone Rifle Corps|Rifle Corps Relationship": "You are or were part of an elite, trusted group that defends Whitestone. Agree with your GM on your current tie to the Rifle Corps. If the campaign starts in Whitestone, you might be an active member. Otherwise, use the Rifle Corps Relationships table for ideas."
};
/* species keys that are the 2014 edition of a species that also exists in the 2024 rules: the 2024 lookup skips them */
export const ONLY14 = ["Aarakocra", "Air Genasi", "Bugbear", "Centaur", "Changeling", "Duergar", "Earth Genasi", "Eladrin", "Firbolg", "Fire Genasi", "Githyanki", "Githzerai", "Goblin", "Goliath", "Hobgoblin", "Kalashtar", "Kenku", "Kobold", "Lizardfolk", "Minotaur", "Orc", "Satyr", "Sea Elf", "Shadar-kai", "Tabaxi", "Tortle", "Triton", "Warforged", "Water Genasi"];
